# Maintainer process

How work on **the starter itself** (koniz-dev/flutter-starter) is tracked,
verified and shipped. It lives on this orphan `maintainers` branch, not on
`main`, because `main` is what an adopter receives from "Use this template",
and none of this belongs to their project. Read it together with `main`'s
`CLAUDE.md`, which describes the code (commands, tests, checks, conventions).

Paths below are relative to this branch. Commands are run from the main
checkout, with:

- `$M` = this worktree, `../flutter-starter-maintainers`
- `$E` = the evidence worktree, `../flutter-starter-evidence`

`scripts/install.sh` sets both up and points the main checkout's untracked
`CLAUDE.local.md` here, so a session loads this file automatically.

## Workflow

GitHub issues are the single source of truth. Full spec:
[`issue-workflow.md`](issue-workflow.md).

1. Labels are authoritative. Any Project board is a read-only mirror.
2. Taxonomy: `type:{bug,feature,task}` (native issue types are org-only and
   unavailable here), one `epic:*`, one `priority:P0..P3`, one `status:*`.
3. States, with every exit and the role that performs it:

   ```
   Backlog (no status label) --planner triage--> status:todo
   status:todo        --implementer claim-->     status:in-progress
   status:in-progress --implementer verifies-->  closed
   status:in-progress --implementer hands off--> status:needs-uat
   any state          --any role, + comment-->   status:blocked
   status:needs-uat   --planner sweep, human PASS--> closed
   status:needs-uat   --planner sweep, human FAIL--> status:todo
   status:blocked     --planner sweep, blocker gone--> status:todo
   status:in-progress --planner sweep, claim is stale--> status:todo
   ```

   No state is a one-way door. `status:blocked` and `status:needs-uat` are
   exited by the **planner** sweep (see `agents/planner.md`), which is
   why the planner runs even when the `status:todo` queue looks non-empty.
4. Exactly one state per open issue, and a `status:*` label is only ever
   **swapped** for another one - never stripped on its own, or the issue lands
   in invisible limbo. **Closing does not touch the label:** the last state
   stays on the closed issue as a record, so `gh issue list --state closed
   --label status:in-progress` is expected to return most closed issues. Always
   pass `--state open` when you mean the live queue. The one legitimate
   no-status issue is an untriaged Backlog issue, open or closed.
5. Every startable issue has a `## Acceptance criteria` section (exact heading)
   with observable steps. No criteria means not startable.
6. Take the top issue from the **Tiebreaker priority order** below - there is
   exactly one such order, and `issue-workflow.md` implements it rather
   than restating it. If the queue is empty, triage one Backlog issue in, then
   restart.
7. Claim by self-assigning and swapping the label, then **re-read the issue** -
   `--add-assignee` adds you alongside an existing assignee rather than failing,
   so this is the only way to know you won the race. If you lost, unassign and
   take the next issue.
8. Commits and PR bodies use `Refs koniz-dev/flutter-starter#N`. `Fixes`,
   `Closes`, and `Resolves` are banned: auto-closing on merge destroys the
   verification gate. It is checked by
   [`scripts/check_issue_refs.sh`](scripts/check_issue_refs.sh) as part of the
   G2 gate. No workflow on `main` runs it any more: an adopter's commits
   reference their own tracker (koniz-dev/flutter-starter#621). Run it before
   pushing: `$M/scripts/check_issue_refs.sh --range origin/main..HEAD`.
9. Ship via feature branch + PR (`CONTRIBUTING.md` naming, Conventional
   Commits), wait for the checks the PR actually gets (see "Which checks a PR
   gets" below), merge `--squash --delete-branch`. `main` is protected:
   Quality gate and the five Strip jobs are required status checks, enforced
   for admins too, so a direct push or a merge on a red PR is rejected. No
   review is required and the other checks (Docs check, Action pins, ...) are
   not required, so reading them is still the session's job.
10. Done means closed AND evidence-backed. One issue at a time.
11. **Dependabot PRs** (`chore(deps)` / `chore(ci)`, from `.github/dependabot.yml`
    on `main`) have no issue and their commits carry no `Refs`. They are
    handled like this, and the refs check is not relaxed for them:
    - **Merge only a PR that needs no code change.** A bump that breaks the
      build, needs regenerated code, or changes behaviour (a new enum value, a
      removed option) gets an issue; the work ships as an ordinary PR that
      `Refs` that issue, and the Dependabot PR is closed as superseded.
      Precedent: #679 split into #692-#694; #707 and #709 superseded by #708
      and #710.
    - **Checks:** the Quality gate and every Strip job green on the current
      head (`gh pr update-branch` first if `main` moved under it), plus the
      `security` phase - a dependency bump is a G3 risk surface - covering
      publisher and lockfile hash. A PR touching `android/` or a native plugin
      also gets `build.yml` dispatched on its branch (#680).
      Do not wait on `gh pr checks --watch`: Dependabot attaches its own
      `.github/dependabot.yml` status, which stays pending, so the watch
      never returns (#700). Poll until every other check has a conclusion.
    - **Squash message:** the maintainer writes the body, so it carries
      `Refs koniz-dev/flutter-starter#<N>` where `<N>` is the issue the bump
      belongs to, or else the Dependabot PR's own number (a PR is linkable
      under the same `#N`; it holds the changelog and the checks). Check it
      with `check_issue_refs.sh --body-file` before merging:

      ```bash
      printf 'Refs koniz-dev/flutter-starter#700\n' > /tmp/body.txt
      $M/scripts/check_issue_refs.sh --body-file /tmp/body.txt
      gh pr merge 700 --squash --delete-branch --body-file /tmp/body.txt
      ```

    - **No issue, no evidence directory.** The PR and its security comment are
      the record. A bump that needed an issue follows the normal G1-G4 path.
12. **Changelog and releases.** An adopter learns what changed from the
    starter's GitHub Releases, not from `main`: their copy of `main` carries
    only the `**Starter version:** vX.Y.Z` line at the top of
    `docs/guides/migration/upgrading-this-starter.md`.
    - Any merged change an adopter could notice (a moved file, a new
      dependency floor, a behaviour change, a new lint) gets an entry under
      `## [Unreleased]` in [`history/starter-changelog.md`](history/starter-changelog.md)
      in the same session that merges it. A **Breaking** entry says what the
      adopter has to change.
    - To release: rename `[Unreleased]` to `[X.Y.Z] - <date>` here, bump the
      marker on `main` through an ordinary PR, then run
      `$M/scripts/release_starter.sh vX.Y.Z --dry-run <commit>`, then the
      same without `--dry-run`. `<commit>` is the `main` commit the changelog
      section describes (default `origin/main`); pass it whenever `main` may
      move between writing the notes and publishing. The script refuses
      unless that commit is on `origin/main` and its marker and the changelog
      heading agree, and publishes the section as the release notes, tagged
      there.

**Canonical epic list:
[`scripts/bootstrap-issue-labels.sh`](scripts/bootstrap-issue-labels.sh)**
(`$M/scripts/bootstrap-issue-labels.sh --list-epics`). Never re-type the epic
list anywhere else - humans, sessions, and any issue-filing integration read it
from that script so the three cannot drift.

The same script also owns the **path -> epic** map:
`$M/scripts/bootstrap-issue-labels.sh --list-map` answers "which epic owns this
diff". Every tracked path maps to exactly one epic, enforced by
[`scripts/check_epic_coverage.dart`](scripts/check_epic_coverage.dart) in the G2
gate. If no epic fits a change, that is a taxonomy gap: file it,
do not force the nearest label - the parallelism rule below keys on that label.

## Acceptance verification

### Scope test: would an adopter notice?

This is a starter template, not an app that ships. A criterion proves the
**mechanism** is correct; it is not there to prove the *product* runs. So before
writing one, ask: **would an adopter notice if this were missing?** If answering
it needs a physical device, a custom CA, an Apple certificate, a reachable
backend or store credentials, that answer belongs to the adopter's app, not to
this repository - prove the mechanism at tier 1 or tier 2 here and hand the
residual over. Precedent: koniz-dev/flutter-starter#48, #59, #62 and #108 each
sat blocked for months of calendar time on exactly that (a real device, a custom
CA, an Apple cert, on-device storage inspection) after the mechanism had already
been proven, and all four closed with the residual assigned to the adopter.

Run `$M/scripts/run_acceptance.sh <issue-number>` from the main checkout. It
runs the format check, `flutter analyze`, `flutter test`, and the golden-tagged
acceptance tests, and tees every log and captured PNG straight into the
evidence worktree's `docs/verification/issue-<N>/` (`$E`, see Evidence
discipline). Nothing it writes lands in the main checkout.

Three tiers exist here:

- **Tier 1 - `flutter test`.** Unit and widget tests on the host VM. Widget
  tests pump real screens via [`test/helpers/pump_app.dart`](test/helpers/pump_app.dart)
  and assert against [`lib/core/constants/ui_keys.dart`](lib/core/constants/ui_keys.dart).
  This is the workhorse.
- **Tier 2 - acceptance goldens.** Tests under `test/acceptance/` tagged
  `golden`, using [`test/acceptance/acceptance_helpers.dart`](test/acceptance/acceptance_helpers.dart).
  [`dart_test.yaml`](dart_test.yaml) skips the `golden` tag by default, so CI,
  `.githooks/pre-push`, and plain `flutter test` ignore them; the acceptance
  runner opts in with `--run-skipped --tags golden`. Goldens are **evidence, not
  a CI gate** - a golden regression will not fail CI, by design.
- **Tier 3 - not drivable here.** Route to `status:needs-uat`.

## Evidence

### Evidence discipline

- Persist artifacts as **committed files** under `docs/verification/issue-<N>/`
  on the orphan **`evidence` branch**, never on `main`: `main` is what an
  adopter gets from "Use this template" (koniz-dev/flutter-starter#619). Keep a
  worktree of it at `$E` (`git worktree add ../flutter-starter-evidence
  origin/evidence`, recipe in that branch's README); `run_acceptance.sh` writes
  there directly. Add anything else the criteria need, commit, and push
  (`git -C $E push origin HEAD:refs/heads/evidence --no-verify`) **before** the
  closing comment, linking
  `https://github.com/koniz-dev/flutter-starter/blob/evidence/docs/verification/issue-<N>/<file>`.
  That branch has no Flutter project, so commit and push there with
  `--no-verify`. Terminal scrollback is not evidence.
- **Nothing on `main` points back here.** Code comments, docs and test names
  say *why* the code is the way it is; they never cite an issue
  (`koniz-dev/flutter-starter#N`, a bare `#N`, an issue URL) or the evidence
  branch. Those belong in commit messages and PR bodies. `check_deliverable.sh`
  fails on the cited forms (koniz-dev/flutter-starter#629).
- **Open every screenshot yourself** and confirm it shows the asserted behavior
  before writing PASS. A zero exit from `run_acceptance.sh` is an input, not a
  verdict; the script prints that reminder on success on purpose.
- **Never relay a subagent's PASS you have not inspected.** If a subagent says a
  screenshot proves something, open that screenshot. A subagent's confidence is
  not evidence and its context is not retrievable later.
- Name the artifact that proves each criterion, one row per criterion. "All
  criteria pass, see logs" is not a PASS summary.
- **Account for the standing goldens.** `run_acceptance.sh` copies every PNG
  under `test/acceptance/goldens/` into the evidence directory, so a non-visual
  issue still ships six screenshots that prove nothing about it. Do not cite
  them. Say so explicitly in the closing comment, backed by a checksum showing
  they are the repository's unchanged goldens:

  ```bash
  shasum -a 256 test/acceptance/goldens/*.png $E/docs/verification/issue-<N>/*.png \
    | sort | tee $E/docs/verification/issue-<N>/goldens-checksums.txt
  ```

  Matching pairs mean the run did not change them and no criterion rests on
  them. A mismatch is a golden regression and needs explaining, not ignoring.
- If a criterion cannot be driven, say which one and why, and route to
  `status:needs-uat` with the exact human steps and what PASS would look like.

## Loop Protocol

Four roles in [`agents/`](agents/) (linked into the checkout's `.claude/agents/` by `scripts/install.sh`): `planner`, `implementer`,
`qa`, `security`. Each role's `tools:` frontmatter scopes its write lane.

**Reviewer roles file issues, they do not fix them.** `qa` and `security` are
declared without `Edit` or `Write`; their output is a filed issue in Backlog.

Caveat, stated plainly: `tools:` gating is coarse. Withholding `Edit`/`Write`
removes the obvious path, but every role needs `Bash` to call `gh`, and `Bash`
can write files. The write lane is therefore enforced by instruction as much as
by capability. If a reviewer role ever edits code, that is a protocol violation
to be caught in review, not something the harness will block.

### Phase order

```
planner  ->  implementer  ->  review (pre-merge, surface-gated)  ->  merge
         ->  qa  ->  security (risk-gated)  ->  close or hand off
```

`security` runs only when the diff touches `lib/core/security/`,
`lib/core/storage/`, `lib/core/network/`, `.env*`, dependency versions, or
anything auth-related. Otherwise skip it.

#### Pre-merge review

A fix verified only against its own issue's criteria can still break the
next thing over. The 2026-10-30 audit traced about 7 of its 69 findings to
fixes merged in the cycle before it, among them a red `main`
(koniz-dev/flutter-starter#935) and a hook that failed every commit in a
monorepo (#936). All of them were visible in the diff. So before merging, a
PR whose diff touches any of these surfaces gets an adversarial review:

- the `security` risk surfaces above, plus `lib/features/auth/` and
  `lib/core/session/`;
- `.githooks/`, `scripts/`, `tool/`, `bricks/`, `.github/workflows/`,
  `fastlane/`.

The reviewer is a fresh subagent (`qa` or `general-purpose`, no worktree
isolation). Its brief holds the PR number, the issue and the diff, but **not
the implementer's own verdict**. It is asked what the change breaks beyond
its criteria: other callers and request shapes, other platforms and shells
(macOS bash 3.2, a monorepo, an adopter's repo rather than the template),
whether each new test fails without the fix, and anything a workflow that
does not run on PRs would catch only after merge.

What it finds gets one of two answers:

- **Fix it on the same branch** when it is a defect of this change.
- **File it** when it is pre-existing or outside the issue's scope.

The PR body names the review and its outcome. A docs-only diff is exempt.

#### Who invokes each phase

No role invokes another role - none of them can. The phase order is driven by
**the session that owns the loop**:

- **Orchestrated run** (a top-level session dispatching role subagents): that
  session is the owner. It dispatches `planner`, then `implementer`, then `qa`
  on the merged change, then `security` if G3 holds. It is also the only party
  that can see two implementers at once, so it enforces Parallelism. This is how
  `qa` came to file koniz-dev/flutter-starter#88 and #89.
- **Solo run** (one session working an issue end to end, no subagents): there is
  nobody to dispatch, so the implementer **performs the `qa` checklist itself**
  before closing, as the last step of G4. The checklist is in
  [`agents/implementer.md`](agents/implementer.md) ("Self-QA
  before closing") and is a copy of steps 2 and 3 of
  [`agents/qa.md`](agents/qa.md).

Self-QA is the weaker of the two - the same session that wrote the PASS is
auditing it. Prefer a separate `qa` pass on anything non-trivial. What is never
acceptable is skipping both and closing anyway.

### Gate table

What must be true before the next phase starts:

| Gate | Before entering | Must be true |
|---|---|---|
| G1 | implementer | Issue has a `## Acceptance criteria` section with observable steps; scope fits one `epic:*`; issue is `status:in-progress` and assigned to this session (re-read confirmed) |
| G1b | merge | The PR's checks are green; if the diff touches a pre-merge review surface, the review ran, its defects are fixed on the branch or filed, and the PR body says so |
| G2 | qa | Change is merged to `main`; commits carry `Refs owner/repo#N` and no `Fixes`/`Closes`/`Resolves` (`$M/scripts/check_issue_refs.sh`); `./scripts/dev/audit_template.sh` exits 0; `dart run $M/scripts/check_epic_coverage.dart` and `$M/scripts/check_deliverable.sh HEAD` exit 0 |
| G3 | security | G2 held and the diff touches a risk surface listed above |
| G4 | close | Every criterion has a named artifact under `docs/verification/issue-<N>/` on the `evidence` branch; every PNG was opened and inspected by the closing session; evidence is committed and pushed; a `qa` pass or the self-QA checklist has run |
| G5 | needs-uat | The blocking criterion is genuinely in tier 3; the comment states the human steps and what PASS means |

A gate that does not hold is not a judgment call. Stop and route.

**The one exception to G4: an issue whose premise is false.** A bug that does
not exist has nothing to verify, so demanding evidence would force a fabricated
PASS table. Close it `--reason "not planned"` with a comment showing *why* the
premise fails - the file and line that already behave correctly, or the command
whose output contradicts the report - and no `docs/verification/` directory.
That is the only close without evidence, and the comment is what makes it
auditable. Precedents: koniz-dev/flutter-starter#25 and #84.

### Escalation

- **QA failure returns to the implementer, not the planner.** QA files an issue
  and comments on the original; the implementer picks it up. The planner is only
  re-entered if the acceptance criteria themselves were wrong or unachievable.
- **Security finding** files a separate issue at `priority:P0` (exploitable) or
  `priority:P1` (hardening) with `epic:core-security`. It never edits code.
- **Scope explosion** (change would span two epics): comment a proposed split,
  set `status:blocked`, unassign. Back to the planner.
- **Missing or unobservable criteria**: `status:blocked` with a comment naming
  what is missing. Back to the planner. Never guess the intent.
- **Lost claim race**: unassign, take the next issue, no comment needed.
- **Stale claim** (a session died holding `status:in-progress`): any role may
  release it - unassign, swap `status:in-progress` back to `status:todo`, and
  comment whether the work had already merged, so the next implementer does not
  redo it. Recipe: "Release a stale claim" in
  [`issue-workflow.md`](issue-workflow.md). Only release a claim you
  have reason to believe is dead; a live claim belongs to its holder.

### Audits

A deep audit fans out read-only lanes and samples the tree; it never covers
all of it. The usual lanes are: the fixes since the last audit, security,
`lib/` correctness, docs against code, and tooling/CI. Point each lane at
the areas the previous audit covered least. Every finding is reproduced and
deduped by the session before anything is filed, as always.

- **P0, P1 and P2 findings** are filed one issue each.
- **P3 findings** go into a single list issue per audit:
  `chore: P3 findings from the <date> audit`, with `type:task`,
  `epic:docs` or `epic:tooling-ci` by majority, and `priority:P3`. Each
  finding is a checkbox carrying its file:line, its evidence and a one-line
  criterion. Its `## Acceptance criteria` is that every box is either ticked
  by a merged change or struck through with a reason. A box that grows into
  real work is promoted to its own issue and linked. Nothing is dropped:
  the list is the filing.
- **Stop rule:** when an audit cycle produces no P0 or P1 finding, deep
  audits stop. Run the next one only when something material changes: a
  starter release, a major dependency or Flutter bump, a new feature slice
  or tool, or a user request. A cycle that still finds a P1 means another
  cycle is due once that cycle's P0/P1 issues are closed.

The 2026-10-30 audit (koniz-dev/flutter-starter#912-#980) predates this rule,
and its 47 P3 findings stay as individual issues.

### Tiebreaker priority order

**This list is the only definition of the order.** `issue-workflow.md`
implements it in its queue selector and does not restate it; nothing else may
paraphrase it. When two candidate issues compete:

1. `priority:P0` over anything else.
2. Unblocks another issue over standalone.
3. Lower `priority:*` number.
4. `type:bug` over `type:feature` over `type:task`.
5. Lower issue number (oldest first).

Levels 1, 3, 4 and 5 are mechanical and the selector sorts by them. Level 2 is
not - no label records "unblocks #N" - so the selector prints the ranked
candidates instead of picking blindly, and the session applies level 2 by
reading them.

### Parallelism

- **Never two implementers in the same `epic:*`.** Merge conflicts in a Clean
  Architecture slice are expensive and the loop cannot arbitrate them.
- One implementer per issue, one issue per implementer. Always.
- `qa` and `security` may run concurrently with each other on the same merged
  change - they only read and file.
- `qa` on issue A may run concurrently with `implementer` on issue B provided
  the epics differ.
- `planner` may run concurrently with anything; triage only writes labels and
  issue bodies.
- Only one session at a time may hold a `status:in-progress` claim on a given
  issue. The claim-then-re-read step in step 7 above is what enforces this.
# CLAUDE.md

Guidance for Claude Code sessions working in this project.

Flutter app built on Clean Architecture: single root `pubspec.yaml`, sources in
`lib/`, tests mirroring them in `test/`, end-to-end flows in `integration_test/`.

## Commands

```bash
flutter pub get
./scripts/dev/audit_template.sh        # codegen + format + analyze + test (CI adds: random order, coverage floor, clean tree)
flutter test --timeout=5m              # unit + widget (goldens skipped by default)
flutter analyze
dart format lib test integration_test tool examples
flutter test --run-skipped --tags golden test/acceptance   # acceptance goldens (tier 2)
```

`flutter pub get` on the current stable leaves the tree clean: the migrations
it used to apply (analyzer excludes, the Pods includes in four `*.xcconfig`
files, the SDK-pinned lock entries) are committed. If it does dirty a tracked file, a new stable re-pinned or re-migrated
something; the Quality gate's "Tracked files are unchanged" step fails on it
too. Fix that on `main` in its own change - re-run `flutter pub get` and commit
the result - rather than reverting it or sweeping it into an unrelated PR.
Always stage explicitly rather than with `git add .`.

Code generation (Freezed, json_serializable, Riverpod) is committed. Regenerate
with `flutter pub run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs` only when
you touch an annotated source.

## Testing

Three tiers:

- **Tier 1 - `flutter test`.** Unit and widget tests on the host VM. Widget
  tests pump real screens via [`test/helpers/pump_app.dart`](test/helpers/pump_app.dart)
  and assert against [`lib/core/constants/ui_keys.dart`](lib/core/constants/ui_keys.dart).
  This is the workhorse.
- **Tier 2 - acceptance goldens.** Tests under `test/acceptance/` tagged
  `golden`, using [`test/acceptance/acceptance_helpers.dart`](test/acceptance/acceptance_helpers.dart).
  [`dart_test.yaml`](dart_test.yaml) skips the `golden` tag by default, so CI,
  `.githooks/pre-push`, and plain `flutter test` ignore them; run them with
  `flutter test --run-skipped --tags golden test/acceptance`. Goldens are a
  record of what screens look like, **not a CI gate** - a golden regression
  will not fail CI, by design.
- **Tier 3 - not drivable on a host.** Real devices, store builds and anything
  needing a live backend; see below.

### What this tooling cannot verify

Be honest about these. Claiming any of them is verified is the fastest way to
make verification worthless.

- **Text content in a golden.** `flutter test` renders text with the Ahem font,
  so **every glyph is an opaque black block**. A golden proves presence,
  position, size, colour, and overflow. It cannot show wording or icon glyphs -
  assert those with `find.text()` / `find.byIcon()`. Never write "the screenshot
  shows the label reads X".
- **Layout with real fonts.** Ahem glyphs are far wider than real ones, so
  goldens wrap and clip text the real app fits on one line. `pumpAcceptance`
  uses a 1200x800 surface to reduce this, but it does not remove it: the
  51-character English `welcome` string measures ~1428px in Ahem at 28px and
  still wraps, versus ~714px in a real font. Treat wrapped or clipped text in a
  golden as a font artifact until you have checked the arithmetic.
- **Patrol E2E.** `integration_test/` and `patrol: ^4.11.0` exist, but
  `patrol_cli` is not installed locally, no Android/iOS device or emulator is
  connected (only macOS desktop and Chrome), and
  [`e2e-android.yml`](.github/workflows/e2e-android.yml) never runs on a
  pull request, by design: it runs on `workflow_dispatch`, plus a weekly
  `schedule` in the template repository only (a repository created from it
  skips the scheduled run). **A session cannot run Patrol locally.**
  Anyone can dispatch it: Actions -> E2E Android (Patrol) -> Run workflow.
  The native harness exists (`android/app/src/androidTest/`,
  `PatrolJUnitRunner`, orchestrator) and run 32966672465 collected and executed a
  real test (`Total: 1`). The job also fails on `Total: 0` or a non-zero
  `Failed:` count, so a green run there is now trustworthy - that was not true
  before, when it went green having run nothing.
  It needs no backend: built without `BASE_URL`, the auth -> home test signs
  in through the offline demo account. One live caveat: `patrol_cli` must
  stay pinned to the version matching the `patrol` dependency, or it aborts
  before any test runs.
- **Real device behavior**, including everything RASP (`lib/core/security/` is a
  no-op by default and only meaningful on a real device).
- **Gestures**: hover, right-click, drag, long-press discoverability,
  multi-touch, one-handed reachability.
- **Store and deploy flows**: `fastlane/`, `deploy-{android,ios,web}.yml` all
  need credentials this environment lacks.
- **Anything needing a live backend.** With no `BASE_URL` the app signs in
  through the offline demo, which proves the flow but not your API; no
  reachable API exists here.
- **Per-layer coverage thresholds.** The Quality gate enforces only a total
  line coverage floor (`COVERAGE_MIN_TOTAL` in [`ci.yml`](.github/workflows/ci.yml)).
  The per-layer targets live in [`coverage.yml`](.github/workflows/coverage.yml),
  which is manual plus weekly, not per-PR.

### Which checks a PR gets

The workflows below run on PRs. **Every PR reports Quality gate** - the
minimum - and Docs check, Action pins, the JDK 25 Android job, Git hooks
and Release scripts join only when their paths change.
While the sample strip is in the tree, every PR also reports its Strip jobs. <!-- strip:strip-tool line -->
`test/tooling/claude_md_checks_table_test.dart` fails if this table and the
workflows' `pull_request` triggers drift apart:

| Workflow | Check name(s) | Runs when |
|---|---|---|
| [`ci.yml`](.github/workflows/ci.yml) | Quality gate | **every** PR; when nothing outside `**/*.md` and `docs/**` changed, format, analyze and the full suite are skipped inside the job and only the tests that read markdown run |
| [`docs-check.yml`](.github/workflows/docs-check.yml) | Docs check | any `**/*.md`, `tool/check_docs.dart`, `tool/doc_signatures.dart`, `tool/doc_symbols.dart`, `tool/doc_env_runs.dart`, `tool/doc_dart_defines.dart`, `tool/doc_test_paths.dart`, `tool/doc_fences.dart`, `lib/core/config/dart_defines.dart`, or the workflow itself (`.github/workflows/docs-check.yml`) |
| [`action-pins.yml`](.github/workflows/action-pins.yml) | Action pins, Workflow lint (actionlint + shellcheck) | any `.github/**` (so every Dependabot github-actions bump) or `tool/check_action_pins.dart`; asks the GitHub API, so it is not part of the offline `flutter test` suite |
| [`build.yml`](.github/workflows/build.yml) | Android on JDK 25 (toolchain floor + debug build) | any `android/**`, `tool/check_android_toolchain.dart`, or the workflow itself (`.github/workflows/build.yml`); its manual Build Android/iOS/Web jobs report as skipped |
| [`git-hooks.yml`](.github/workflows/git-hooks.yml) | pre-commit hook in a fresh clone | any `.githooks/**`, `scripts/test/pre_commit_hook_test.sh`, or the workflow itself (`.github/workflows/git-hooks.yml`); in a repository created from the template, the job reports as skipped |
| [`release-scripts.yml`](.github/workflows/release-scripts.yml) | release scripts (ubuntu-latest), release scripts (macos-latest) | any `scripts/ci/generate_changelog.sh`, `scripts/ci/release.sh`, `scripts/ci/bump_version.sh`, `scripts/test/release_scripts_test.sh`, or the workflow itself (`.github/workflows/release-scripts.yml`); in a repository created from the template, the jobs report as skipped |
| [`strip-smoke.yml`](.github/workflows/strip-smoke.yml) | Strip `<variant>` + analyze + test (x5) | **every** PR in the template repository (in a repository created from it, the job reports as skipped); strip, analyze and test are skipped inside each job when nothing outside `**/*.md` and `docs/**` changed <!-- strip:strip-tool line --> |

`ci.yml` has no `paths-ignore`; it filters inside the job instead ("Decide
the gate scope"), so the check always posts a conclusion and can be a required status check, while
a docs-only PR runs only the tests that read markdown (`DOC_TESTS` in `ci.yml`;
`test/tooling/ci_doc_tests_cover_markdown_readers_test.dart` keeps that list
complete).
`strip-smoke.yml` does the same ("Decide the strip scope"). <!-- strip:strip-tool line --> A PR reporting zero checks is the
registration race, never a path exclusion. Poll until
`gh pr checks --json name --jq 'length'` is non-zero before
`gh pr checks --watch`; merging on the "no checks reported" reading skips the
gate entirely.

**Read a green Quality gate or Strip check carefully on a docs-only PR.** It
does not mean format, analyze and the full suite passed - on the Quality gate
only the tests that read markdown ran, and on Strip nothing ran, because no
other changed path could have affected them. Each job summary states which
of the two happened and lists every changed path. The skip set is `**/*.md` anywhere plus `docs/**`,
minus `.dart`: `analysis_options.yaml` does not exclude `docs/`, so a `.dart`
file committed under `docs/` is analyzed like any other source and forces the
full gate.

**None of these checks gates a merge until you turn on branch protection.**
Without it, `gh pr merge --squash` succeeds on a red PR and a direct push to
`main` is not rejected, so reading the checks is the session's job. Quality
gate always posts a conclusion, so it is safe to list in
`required_status_checks.contexts` when you enable protection.

## Conventions

- Clean Architecture: domain -> data -> presentation. Business logic stays in
  domain; contracts in `lib/core/contracts` with adapters alongside.
- Riverpod for DI and state; `very_good_analysis` lint rules.
- Conventional Commits enforced by `.githooks/commit-msg`. Install hooks with
  `./scripts/dev/setup_git_hooks.sh`.
- Tests mirror `lib/`. New feature slices: `mason make feature_clean` or
  `./scripts/dev/create_feature.sh`.
<!-- strip:strip-tool start -->
- `tool/golden/` mirrors `lib/` for `strip_sample_features.dart`. Changing a
  file that has a `tool/golden/*` counterpart means updating that counterpart too,
  or [`strip-smoke.yml`](.github/workflows/strip-smoke.yml) breaks.
<!-- strip:strip-tool end -->
- No emoji in `docs/`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `.claude/`. The existing `README.md` and
  `CONTRIBUTING.md` use them; leave those alone unless the issue is about them.
// Transport facade. Its public request surface is typed entirely on
// `lib/core/contracts/network_contracts.dart`, so a caller can hold an
// ApiClient without importing `package:dio`.
// ignore_for_file: directives_ordering

import 'package:dio/dio.dart';
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/config/app_config.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/contracts/network_contracts.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/constants/api_endpoints.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/errors/exceptions.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/logging/logging_service.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/adapters/dio_network_client.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/request_cancellation.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/api_logging_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/auth_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/cache_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/error_interceptor.dart';

import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/performance_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/retry_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/ssl_pinning.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/performance/i_performance_service.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/storage/secure_storage_service.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/storage/storage_service.dart';

/// API client for making HTTP requests
class ApiClient {
  /// Creates an instance of [ApiClient] with configured Dio instance
  ///
  /// [storageService] - Storage service for non-sensitive data
  /// [secureStorageService] - Secure storage service for authentication
  /// tokens
  /// [authInterceptor] - Auth interceptor for token management and refresh
  /// [loggingService] - Optional logging service; when set, adds
  /// [ApiLoggingInterceptor] for HTTP logging.
  /// [performanceService] - Optional performance service for automatic HTTP
  /// request tracking
  /// [sslPinning] - Certificate pinning policy; defaults to
  /// [SslPinning.fromConfig] (`ENABLE_SSL_PINNING` / `API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS`)
  /// [cacheConfig] - `CacheInterceptor` freshness policy; defaults to
  /// [CacheConfig]'s one hour fresh, seven days as a fallback
  /// [retryInitialDelay] and [retryMaxJitter] - Override
  /// [RetryInterceptor]'s backoff, so a test that walks the retry path does
  /// not sleep through it
  /// [releaseMode] lets host tests exercise the native release HTTPS guard.
  ApiClient({
    required StorageService storageService,
    required SecureStorageService secureStorageService,
    required AuthInterceptor authInterceptor,
    LoggingService? loggingService,
    IPerformanceService? performanceService,
    SslPinning? sslPinning,
    CacheConfig? cacheConfig,
    @visibleForTesting Duration? retryInitialDelay,
    @visibleForTesting Duration? retryMaxJitter,
    @visibleForTesting bool releaseMode = kReleaseMode,
  }) : _dio = _createDio(
         storageService,
         secureStorageService,
         authInterceptor,
         loggingService,
         performanceService,
         sslPinning ?? SslPinning.fromConfig(),
         cacheConfig,
         retryInitialDelay,
         retryMaxJitter,
         releaseMode,
       ) {
    _networkClient = DioNetworkClient(
      _dio,
      deadline: Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiTimeout),
    );
    _cacheInterceptor = _dio.interceptors.whereType<CacheInterceptor>().single;
  }

  static Dio _createDio(
    StorageService storageService,
    SecureStorageService secureStorageService,
    AuthInterceptor authInterceptor,
    LoggingService? loggingService,
    IPerformanceService? performanceService,
    SslPinning sslPinning,
    CacheConfig? cacheConfig,
    Duration? retryInitialDelay,
    Duration? retryMaxJitter,
    bool releaseMode,
  ) {
    final dio = Dio(
      BaseOptions(
        baseUrl: AppConfig.baseUrl + ApiEndpoints.apiVersion,
        connectTimeout: Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiConnectTimeout),
        receiveTimeout: Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiReceiveTimeout),
        sendTimeout: Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiSendTimeout),
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
          'Accept': 'application/json',
        },
      ),
    );

    // SSL Pinning Configuration.
    //
    // Native staging/production releases guard every redirect hop against
    // cleartext. Pinning remains independent of that scheme requirement;
    // requesting pins without fingerprints logs and asserts eagerly.
    final pinnedAdapter = sslPinning.createAdapter(
      requireHttps:
          releaseMode && (AppConfig.isStaging || AppConfig.isProduction),
    );
    if (pinnedAdapter != null) {
      dio.httpClientAdapter = pinnedAdapter;
    }

    // Let the 401 replay client borrow this transport, so a retry carrying a
    // freshly minted access token goes through the same pinned adapter
    // instead of a bare, system-trust-store Dio.
    authInterceptor.attachTransport(dio);

    // Shares AuthInterceptor's token store on purpose: the cache must know
    // whether a credential exists, and it cannot learn that from the request
    // headers, which AuthInterceptor only writes at its own position in the
    // chain. See CacheInterceptor's class doc.
    final cacheInterceptor = CacheInterceptor(
      storageService: storageService,
      tokenStore: authInterceptor.tokenStore,
      cacheConfig: cacheConfig,
    );

    // A forced logout (401 whose refresh fails) has to empty the same cache
    // `AuthRepositoryImpl.logout()` empties, or the session boundary the user
    // did not choose leaves cached bodies behind.
    authInterceptor.attachResponseCache(cacheInterceptor);

    // Add interceptors - Order matters!
    //
    // dio runs `onRequest` in list order AND `onError` in list order (see
    // `dio_mixin.dart`: both loops iterate `interceptors` forwards). An
    // `onError` that calls `handler.reject(...)` terminates the error chain,
    // so every interceptor registered after it never sees the error.
    // ErrorInterceptor does exactly that, therefore it must be registered
    // LAST - registering it first silently disabled retry, 401 token refresh,
    // performance trace teardown and error logging.
    //
    // CacheInterceptor should be early to intercept requests before network
    // AuthInterceptor handles token injection and 401 refresh
    // RetryInterceptor retries transient failures (timeouts, 5xx)
    // ApiLoggingInterceptor logs a redacted stand-in for the DioException
    // (omitted if loggingService is null)
    // PerformanceInterceptor sits after Auth and Retry so its trace records
    // how the request finally ended - a refreshed-and-replayed 401 is the
    // replay's 200, not a failed 401
    // (omitted if performanceService is null)
    // ErrorInterceptor converts whatever survives into a domain exception
    dio.interceptors.addAll([
      cacheInterceptor,
      authInterceptor,
      RetryInterceptor(
        dio: dio,
        loggingService: loggingService,
        initialExecutionDelay: retryInitialDelay ?? const Duration(seconds: 1),
        maxJitter: retryMaxJitter ?? const Duration(milliseconds: 500),
      ),
      if (loggingService != null)
        ApiLoggingInterceptor(loggingService: loggingService),
      if (performanceService != null)
        PerformanceInterceptor(performanceService: performanceService),
      ErrorInterceptor(),
    ]);

    return dio;
  }

  final Dio _dio;
  late final DioNetworkClient _networkClient;
  late final CacheInterceptor _cacheInterceptor;

  /// Escape hatch onto the underlying Dio instance.
  ///
  /// Deliberately kept, and deliberately not on any request path. No verb on
  /// this class accepts or returns a dio type, so reaching for
  /// `.dio` is an explicit opt-out of [INetworkClient] rather than something
  /// an ordinary `post()` forces on the caller. Use it for transport-level
  /// concerns only - swapping [Dio.httpClientAdapter], inspecting
  /// [Dio.interceptors], reading [BaseOptions]. Typing a data source on what
  /// it returns puts `package:dio` back in the feature layer, which is the
  /// leak the typed verbs closed.
  Dio get dio => _dio;

  /// Getter for transport-agnostic network contract.
  INetworkClient get networkClient => _networkClient;

  /// The locally persisted HTTP response cache installed on this client.
  ///
  /// Session teardown calls `clearCache()` on it so cached bodies do not
  /// survive a logout.
  IHttpResponseCache get responseCache => _cacheInterceptor;

  /// GET request
  ///
  /// [path] - The endpoint path
  /// [queryParameters] - Optional query parameters
  /// [headers] - Optional per-request headers
  /// [cancellation] - Optional handle that cancels the request
  /// Returns a [Future] that completes with a [NetworkResponse]
  /// Throws domain exceptions (ServerException, NetworkException, etc.)
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> get(
    String path, {
    Map<String, dynamic>? queryParameters,
    Map<String, String>? headers,
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    return await _send(
      cancellation: cancellation,
      NetworkRequest(
        path: path,
        method: NetworkMethod.get,
        queryParameters: queryParameters ?? const <String, dynamic>{},
        headers: headers ?? const <String, String>{},
      ),
    );
  }

  /// POST request
  ///
  /// [path] - The endpoint path
  /// [data] - Optional request body data
  /// [queryParameters] - Optional query parameters
  /// [headers] - Optional per-request headers
  /// [cancellation] - Optional handle that cancels the request
  /// Returns a [Future] that completes with a [NetworkResponse]
  /// Throws domain exceptions (ServerException, NetworkException, etc.)
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> post(
    String path, {
    dynamic data,
    Map<String, dynamic>? queryParameters,
    Map<String, String>? headers,
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    return await _send(
      cancellation: cancellation,
      NetworkRequest(
        path: path,
        method: NetworkMethod.post,
        body: data,
        queryParameters: queryParameters ?? const <String, dynamic>{},
        headers: headers ?? const <String, String>{},
      ),
    );
  }

  /// PUT request
  ///
  /// [path] - The endpoint path
  /// [data] - Optional request body data
  /// [queryParameters] - Optional query parameters
  /// [headers] - Optional per-request headers
  /// [cancellation] - Optional handle that cancels the request
  /// Returns a [Future] that completes with a [NetworkResponse]
  /// Throws domain exceptions (ServerException, NetworkException, etc.)
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> put(
    String path, {
    dynamic data,
    Map<String, dynamic>? queryParameters,
    Map<String, String>? headers,
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    return await _send(
      cancellation: cancellation,
      NetworkRequest(
        path: path,
        method: NetworkMethod.put,
        body: data,
        queryParameters: queryParameters ?? const <String, dynamic>{},
        headers: headers ?? const <String, String>{},
      ),
    );
  }

  /// PATCH request
  ///
  /// [path] - The endpoint path
  /// [data] - Optional request body data
  /// [queryParameters] - Optional query parameters
  /// [headers] - Optional per-request headers
  /// [cancellation] - Optional handle that cancels the request
  /// Returns a [Future] that completes with a [NetworkResponse]
  /// Throws domain exceptions (ServerException, NetworkException, etc.)
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> patch(
    String path, {
    dynamic data,
    Map<String, dynamic>? queryParameters,
    Map<String, String>? headers,
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    return await _send(
      cancellation: cancellation,
      NetworkRequest(
        path: path,
        method: NetworkMethod.patch,
        body: data,
        queryParameters: queryParameters ?? const <String, dynamic>{},
        headers: headers ?? const <String, String>{},
      ),
    );
  }

  /// DELETE request
  ///
  /// [path] - The endpoint path
  /// [data] - Optional request body data
  /// [queryParameters] - Optional query parameters
  /// [headers] - Optional per-request headers
  /// [cancellation] - Optional handle that cancels the request
  /// Returns a [Future] that completes with a [NetworkResponse]
  /// Throws domain exceptions (ServerException, NetworkException, etc.)
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> delete(
    String path, {
    dynamic data,
    Map<String, dynamic>? queryParameters,
    Map<String, String>? headers,
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    return await _send(
      cancellation: cancellation,
      NetworkRequest(
        path: path,
        method: NetworkMethod.delete,
        body: data,
        queryParameters: queryParameters ?? const <String, dynamic>{},
        headers: headers ?? const <String, String>{},
      ),
    );
  }

  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> _send(
    NetworkRequest request, {
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    try {
      return await _networkClient.send(request, cancellation: cancellation);
    } on AppException {
      rethrow;
    } on Exception catch (e) {
      throw NetworkException(e.toString());
    }
  }
}
import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:math';

import 'package:dio/dio.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/logging/logging_service.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/api_logging_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/request_body_replay.dart';

/// Interceptor to automatically retry failed network requests
///
/// Uses an exponential backoff strategy with jitter for transient errors
/// (timeouts, connection errors, and 5xx server errors).
///
/// ## Which requests are replayed
///
/// Replaying a request the server may already have processed turns one user
/// action into several server-side writes, so the HTTP method is part of the
/// decision:
///
/// * **Safe methods** ([safeMethods]: `GET`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`; RFC 9110
///   section 9.2.1) have no server-side effect, so an extra attempt costs
///   nothing. They are retried on every transient error type.
/// * **Everything else** (`POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`, ...) is retried
///   only on [DioExceptionType.connectionTimeout] - the one dio error type
///   that proves no request byte reached the server, because the socket never
///   finished connecting. A `sendTimeout`, a `receiveTimeout`, a
///   `connectionError` (a socket can also be reset *after* the body was sent)
///   or a 5xx from an intermediary all leave open the possibility that the
///   write was committed upstream.
///
/// `PUT` and `DELETE` are idempotent but not safe, so they are not replayed by
/// default either: idempotency promises only that the server-side *state* is
/// unchanged by a repeat, not that the repeat is harmless - a replayed
/// `DELETE` answers 404 and a replayed `PUT` can clobber a concurrent write.
/// Opt those in per request when the endpoint really is replay-safe.
///
/// ## Per-request override
///
/// Two equivalent, explicit, per-request opt-ins exist, both off by default:
///
/// ```dart
/// // Wherever a raw Dio call is available:
/// dio.post('/orders', data: body, options: Options(extra: {'retry': true}));
///
/// // Through ApiClient, whose facade forwards headers but not `extra`:
/// apiClient.post(
///   '/orders',
///   data: body,
///   headers: {RetryInterceptor.idempotencyKeyHeader: key},
/// );
/// ```
///
/// `extra: {'retry': false}` is the mirror image and suppresses retry even for
/// a safe method. An override only decides whether *this request* may be
/// replayed; the error-type and [maxRetries] gates still apply.
class RetryInterceptor extends Interceptor {
  /// Creates a [RetryInterceptor]
  RetryInterceptor({
    required this.dio,
    this.loggingService,
    this.maxRetries = 3,
    this.initialExecutionDelay = const Duration(seconds: 1),
    this.maxJitter = const Duration(milliseconds: 500),
  });

  /// HTTP methods replayed automatically on any transient error.
  ///
  /// The *safe* methods of RFC 9110 section 9.2.1: read-only, so an extra
  /// attempt cannot duplicate a server-side effect.
  static const Set<String> safeMethods = {'GET', 'HEAD', 'OPTIONS'};

  /// `RequestOptions.extra` key carrying the per-request override.
  ///
  /// `true` allows replaying an otherwise non-replayable request; `false`
  /// suppresses replay entirely. Absent means "decide from the method".
  static const String retryExtraKey = 'retry';

  /// Request header that opts a non-safe request into replay.
  ///
  /// Sending an idempotency key is the caller telling the server to
  /// de-duplicate, which is precisely what makes a replay safe. Matched
  /// case-insensitively; an empty value does not opt in.
  static const String idempotencyKeyHeader = 'Idempotency-Key';

  /// The Dio instance used for retrying requests
  final Dio dio;

  /// Optional logging service for tracking retry attempts
  final LoggingService? loggingService;

  /// Maximum number of retries per request
  final int maxRetries;

  /// Initial delay before the first retry (will exponentially backoff)
  final Duration initialExecutionDelay;

  /// Upper bound (exclusive) of the random delay added to every retry, so
  /// clients that failed together do not retry in lockstep.
  /// [Duration.zero] disables it.
  final Duration maxJitter;

  final Random _rnd = Random();

  @override
  Future<void> onError(
    DioException err,
    ErrorInterceptorHandler handler,
  ) async {
    final extra = err.requestOptions.extra;
    var retryCount = extra['retry_count'] as int? ?? 0;

    // A body that can be read only once cannot be sent again. Not retrying
    // leaves the caller with the real failure rather than a StateError from
    // the replay.
    if (!RequestBodyReplay.canReplay(err.requestOptions.data)) {
      if (_shouldRetry(err) && retryCount < maxRetries) {
        loggingService?.debug(
          'Not retrying '
          '[${ApiLoggingInterceptor.redactUrl(err.requestOptions.path)}]: '
          '${RequestBodyReplay.reason(err.requestOptions.data)}',
        );
      }
      return super.onError(err, handler);
    }

    if (_shouldRetry(err) && retryCount < maxRetries) {
      retryCount++;
      err.requestOptions.extra['retry_count'] = retryCount;

      final delay = _getDelay(retryCount);

      loggingService?.debug(
        'Retrying request '
        '[${ApiLoggingInterceptor.redactUrl(err.requestOptions.path)}] '
        '(attempt $retryCount of $maxRetries) in ${delay.inMilliseconds}ms',
      );

      await Future<void>.delayed(delay);

      try {
        final options = err.requestOptions;

        final response = await dio.request<dynamic>(
          options.path,
          // A FormData is finalized by its first send; send a clone.
          data: RequestBodyReplay.bodyFor(options.data),
          queryParameters: options.queryParameters,
          cancelToken: options.cancelToken,
          options: Options(
            method: options.method,
            headers: options.headers,
            responseType: options.responseType,
            contentType: options.contentType,
            validateStatus: options.validateStatus,
            followRedirects: options.followRedirects,
            maxRedirects: options.maxRedirects,
            receiveTimeout: options.receiveTimeout,
            sendTimeout: options.sendTimeout,
            transformTimeout: options.transformTimeout,
            extra: options.extra,
          ),
          onReceiveProgress: options.onReceiveProgress,
          onSendProgress: options.onSendProgress,
        );
        return handler.resolve(response);
      } on DioException catch (e) {
        // If the retry also fails it will re-enter the interceptor pipeline
        // via dio.request. But for safety against infinite loops that
        // bypass the extra map, we pass it down. Actually, dio.request
        // above triggers interceptors again from the start, so it will
        // naturally hit RetryInterceptor again with the incremented
        // retryCount. No need to call super.onError here as the new
        // request handles its own interceptors.
        return handler.reject(e);
      }
    }

    return super.onError(err, handler);
  }

  bool _shouldRetry(DioException err) {
    final options = err.requestOptions;
    final override = _replayOverride(options);

    if (override == false) {
      return false;
    }

    final replayable =
        override ?? safeMethods.contains(options.method.toUpperCase());

    if (!replayable) {
      // The request may have been received and committed by the server, so a
      // replay is only safe when the failure proves it never got there.
      // connectionTimeout is the only dio error type carrying that proof.
      return err.type == DioExceptionType.connectionTimeout;
    }

    return _isTransient(err);
  }

  /// Per-request opt-in/opt-out, or null when the caller said nothing.
  bool? _replayOverride(RequestOptions options) {
    final flag = options.extra[retryExtraKey];
    if (flag is bool) {
      return flag;
    }

    for (final entry in options.headers.entries) {
      if (entry.key.toLowerCase() == idempotencyKeyHeader.toLowerCase()) {
        final value = entry.value?.toString() ?? '';
        if (value.isNotEmpty) {
          return true;
        }
      }
    }

    return null;
  }

  bool _isTransient(DioException err) {
    if (err.type == DioExceptionType.connectionTimeout ||
        err.type == DioExceptionType.sendTimeout ||
        err.type == DioExceptionType.receiveTimeout ||
        err.type == DioExceptionType.connectionError) {
      return true;
    }

    if (err.type == DioExceptionType.badResponse) {
      final statusCode = err.response?.statusCode;
      if (statusCode != null && (statusCode >= 500 && statusCode <= 599)) {
        return true;
      }
    }

    return false;
  }

  Duration _getDelay(int retryCount) {
    final jitter = maxJitter > Duration.zero
        ? Duration(microseconds: _rnd.nextInt(maxJitter.inMicroseconds))
        : Duration.zero;
    final delay = Duration(
      milliseconds:
          initialExecutionDelay.inMilliseconds * pow(2, retryCount - 1).toInt(),
    );
    return delay + jitter;
  }
}
import 'dart:async';

// Constructor preserves backward-compatible parameter ordering/signature.
// ignore_for_file: always_put_required_named_parameters_first, lines_longer_than_80_chars

import 'package:dio/dio.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/config/app_config.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/constants/api_endpoints.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/constants/app_constants.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/contracts/network_contracts.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/contracts/state_boundary_contracts.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/contracts/storage_contracts.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/errors/exceptions.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/errors/failures.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/adapters/shared_transport_adapter.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/api_logging_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/performance_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/retry_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/request_body_replay.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/ssl_pinning.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/session/session_generation.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/storage/adapters/secure_token_store.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/storage/secure_storage_service.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/utils/result.dart';

/// Pending request data structure for queuing requests during token refresh
class _PendingRequest {
  _PendingRequest(this.error, this.handler);

  final DioException error;
  final ErrorInterceptorHandler handler;
}

/// Interceptor for adding authentication tokens to requests and handling
/// automatic token refresh on 401 Unauthorized responses
class AuthInterceptor extends Interceptor {
  /// Creates an [AuthInterceptor] with the given dependencies
  AuthInterceptor({
    ITokenStore? tokenStore,
    SecureStorageService? secureStorageService,
    required Future<Result<String>> Function() refreshToken,
    Dio Function()? retryDioFactory,
    IKeyValueStore? keyValueStore,
    ISessionTerminationSink? sessionSink,
    SessionGeneration? sessionGeneration,
  }) : _retryDioFactory = retryDioFactory,
       _keyValueStore = keyValueStore,
       _sessionSink = sessionSink,
       _sessionGeneration = sessionGeneration ?? SessionGeneration(),
       _tokenStore =
           tokenStore ??
           (secureStorageService != null
               ? SecureTokenStore(secureStorageService)
               : throw ArgumentError(
                   'Either tokenStore or secureStorageService must be provided.',
                 )),
       _refreshToken = refreshToken;

  /// Token storage for retrieving and storing authentication tokens
  final ITokenStore _tokenStore;

  /// The token store this interceptor authenticates from.
  ///
  /// Exposed so other interceptors can make credential-dependent decisions
  /// from the same source of truth instead of inspecting request headers,
  /// which are only populated once [onRequest] has run.
  ITokenStore get tokenStore => _tokenStore;

  /// Non-sensitive storage holding the cached user blob, if wired.
  ///
  /// Needed so a forced logout here leaves the same state as
  /// `AuthRepository.logout()`. Optional only so existing call sites that
  /// construct this interceptor by hand keep compiling; the app wires it in
  /// `lib/features/auth/di/auth_providers.dart`.
  final IKeyValueStore? _keyValueStore;

  /// The running app's session state, if wired.
  ///
  /// Clearing storage is not enough on its own: the app that is *already
  /// running* holds the session in memory, and nothing in this layer is
  /// allowed to know what holds it. A forced logout that only emptied storage
  /// left the authenticated shell on screen, backed by credentials that no
  /// longer existed, until the next cold start.
  ///
  /// A constructor dependency rather than an `attach...` call, unlike
  /// [_responseCache]: the sink is resolvable when `authInterceptorProvider`
  /// builds this interceptor, because it defers reading the notifier until
  /// something actually terminates a session.
  ///
  /// Optional for the same reason [_keyValueStore] is: hand-built call sites
  /// (and every test that drives this interceptor standalone) keep compiling,
  /// and a missing sink degrades to not telling the running app - the
  /// behaviour before the sink existed - rather than to a crash.
  final ISessionTerminationSink? _sessionSink;

  /// Which sign-in the credentials this interceptor writes belong to.
  ///
  /// The 401 handler reads it before awaiting the refresh and re-checks it
  /// before persisting anything, so a logout that lands during the round trip
  /// cannot be undone by the response that arrives after it.
  /// [_logoutUser] advances it for the same
  /// reason in the other direction.
  ///
  /// Shared with `AuthRepositoryImpl` through `sessionGenerationProvider` in
  /// production: repository refreshes persist their own credentials, and a
  /// per-instance counter would leave those writes unguarded.
  ///
  /// Optional for the same reason [_keyValueStore] and [_sessionSink] are:
  /// hand-built call sites keep compiling. A standalone interceptor still
  /// guards its own writes against its own forced logouts; it simply cannot
  /// see terminations raised elsewhere, because nothing else holds its
  /// counter.
  final SessionGeneration _sessionGeneration;

  /// Locally persisted HTTP response cache, if wired.
  ///
  /// Set by `ApiClient` through [attachResponseCache] rather than by the
  /// constructor: the cache is a `CacheInterceptor` the client builds itself,
  /// so it does not exist yet when this interceptor is constructed in
  /// `authInterceptorProvider`.
  ///
  /// Stays null when nobody attaches one, which mirrors how `httpCache` is
  /// optional in `AuthRepositoryImpl`.
  IHttpResponseCache? _responseCache;

  /// Binds the response cache this interceptor must empty on forced logout.
  ///
  /// Called by `ApiClient` once the cache interceptor is built. Safe to call
  /// more than once; the last cache wins.
  ///
  /// A method rather than the setter `use_setters_to_change_properties` asks
  /// for, to pair with [attachTransport]: both are post-construction wiring
  /// performed by `ApiClient`, and reading them the same way is worth more here
  /// than the lint.
  // ignore: use_setters_to_change_properties
  void attachResponseCache(IHttpResponseCache cache) => _responseCache = cache;

  /// Callback to refresh the access token.
  ///
  /// Intentionally a callback (instead of depending on a repository/provider)
  /// to avoid DI cycles during app bootstrap.
  final Future<Result<String>> Function() _refreshToken;

  /// Optional factory for the Dio instance used to replay the original
  /// request after a successful refresh.
  ///
  /// Production leaves this null: the replay client is built once and borrows
  /// the transport of the client this interceptor is attached to (see
  /// [attachTransport]), so the replay cannot re-enter this interceptor but
  /// still goes through the pinned adapter. Tests inject a factory to point
  /// the replay at a fake [HttpClientAdapter].
  final Dio Function()? _retryDioFactory;

  /// The client this interceptor is installed on, if any.
  ///
  /// Only its transport and base options are borrowed - never its interceptor
  /// chain, which would re-enter this interceptor on every replay.
  Dio? _hostDio;

  /// Single replay client, created on first use and reused afterwards.
  Dio? _retryDio;

  /// Binds this interceptor to the client it is installed on.
  ///
  /// Called by `ApiClient` after the transport (including certificate
  /// pinning) is configured, so replayed requests use the same adapter
  /// instead of a fresh, unpinned one.
  void attachTransport(Dio dio) {
    _hostDio = dio;
    // Drop any replay client built against the previous transport.
    _closeRetryDio();
  }

  /// Returns the single replay client, creating it on first use.
  Dio retryDio() {
    final existing = _retryDio;
    if (existing != null) {
      return existing;
    }

    final factory = _retryDioFactory;
    final created = factory != null ? factory() : _buildRetryDio();
    _retryDio = created;
    return created;
  }

  Dio _buildRetryDio() {
    final host = _hostDio;
    final dio = Dio(
      BaseOptions(
        baseUrl:
            host?.options.baseUrl ??
            AppConfig.baseUrl + ApiEndpoints.apiVersion,
        connectTimeout:
            host?.options.connectTimeout ??
            Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiConnectTimeout),
        receiveTimeout:
            host?.options.receiveTimeout ??
            Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiReceiveTimeout),
        sendTimeout:
            host?.options.sendTimeout ??
            Duration(seconds: AppConfig.apiSendTimeout),
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
          'Accept': 'application/json',
        },
      ),
    );

    if (host != null) {
      // Borrow the host transport so pinning applies to the replay too.
      dio.httpClientAdapter = SharedTransportAdapter(
        () => host.httpClientAdapter,
      );
      // And the host's logging and performance tracing, in the host's
      // order: a replay resolved from onError ends the host chain, so only
      // these see its response. ApiLoggingInterceptor redacts the replay's
      // fresh Authorization header like any other.
      dio.interceptors.addAll(
        host.interceptors.where(
          (i) => i is ApiLoggingInterceptor || i is PerformanceInterceptor,
        ),
      );
    } else {
      // Not attached to a client (standalone use): pin the replay directly.
      final pinned = SslPinning.fromConfig().createAdapter();
      if (pinned != null) {
        dio.httpClientAdapter = pinned;
      }
    }

    return dio;
  }

  void _closeRetryDio() {
    _retryDio?.close(force: true);
    _retryDio = null;
  }

  /// Releases the replay client and its connection pool.
  ///
  /// Wired to provider disposal; safe to call more than once.
  void dispose() => _closeRetryDio();

  /// Flag to track if token refresh is in progress
  bool _isRefreshing = false;

  /// Queue of pending requests waiting for token refresh to complete
  final List<_PendingRequest> _pendingRequests = [];

  /// `RequestOptions.extra` key recording that the `Authorization` header was
  /// written by [onRequest] rather than attached by the caller.
  static const String _injectedAuthorizationKey = 'auth_interceptor_injected';

  /// The failure code a refresher sets when the refresh token itself is dead.
  ///
  /// The same string as `AuthErrorCodes.refreshTokenExpired`, which core
  /// cannot import; a test pins the two
  /// together. See [_endsSession].
  static const String refreshTokenExpiredCode = 'REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRED';

  /// `RequestOptions.extra` key recording the [SessionGeneration] a request
  /// was first sent under. See [onRequest].
  static const String _issuedUnderGenerationKey = 'auth_session_generation';

  /// Endpoints that should not trigger token refresh
  static const List<String> _excludedEndpoints = [
    ApiEndpoints.login,
    ApiEndpoints.register,
    ApiEndpoints.refreshToken,
    ApiEndpoints.logout,
  ];

  @override
  Future<void> onRequest(
    RequestOptions options,
    RequestInterceptorHandler handler,
  ) async {
    // An absolute URL on another origin - a presigned upload, a CDN, a
    // pagination link - is not this API: the token is never sent there, and
    // a header the caller attached is theirs to keep.
    if (!_targetsApiOrigin(options)) return super.onRequest(options, handler);

    // A re-send - a `RetryInterceptor` attempt runs the whole chain again with
    // the earlier attempt's `extra` - of a request first sent under a session
    // that has since ended. Authorising it with the token stored now would
    // send it as whoever signed in during the backoff, and hand that user's
    // response to the ended session's caller.
    // The first send stamps the generation, so only a re-send can find it
    // stale here; the check after the token read covers both.
    final issuedUnder = options.extra.putIfAbsent(
      _issuedUnderGenerationKey,
      () => _sessionGeneration.current,
    );
    if (_rejectIfSessionEnded(issuedUnder, options, handler)) return;

    // Get token from secure storage. dio does not await this callback, so a
    // store that throws - `ITokenStore` is a public seam, and an `Error` gets
    // past the default store's own guard - would call neither `next` nor
    // `reject`: the caller's future would never complete, and the exception
    // would surface as an uncaught zone error.
    final String? token;
    try {
      token = await _tokenStore.getAccessToken();
    } on Object catch (error, stackTrace) {
      options.extra[RetryInterceptor.retryExtraKey] = false;
      handler.reject(
        DioException(
          requestOptions: options,
          error: error,
          stackTrace: stackTrace,
          message: 'Could not read the access token.',
        ),
        true,
      );
      return;
    }

    // Checked again because the read above is a platform call: a sign-out and
    // a different user's sign-in landing while it was pending return *their*
    // token, and sending it would run this session's request - first send or
    // re-send - as the new user.
    if (_rejectIfSessionEnded(issuedUnder, options, handler)) return;

    if (token != null && token.isNotEmpty) {
      options.headers['Authorization'] = 'Bearer $token';
      options.extra[_injectedAuthorizationKey] = true;
    } else if (options.extra.remove(_injectedAuthorizationKey) == true) {
      // A re-send of a request this interceptor already authorised - a
      // `RetryInterceptor` attempt copies the earlier attempt's headers - and
      // the session has ended since. Leaving the header would put the ended
      // session's bearer token on the wire.
      // Only a header this interceptor wrote is removed; one the caller
      // attached itself is theirs to keep.
      options.headers.remove('Authorization');
    }

    super.onRequest(options, handler);
  }

  /// Rejects [options] when the session it was [issuedUnder] has ended.
  ///
  /// Returns true when it rejected, so the caller stops. The rejection is a
  /// `DioExceptionType.cancel` marked with [RetryInterceptor.retryExtraKey]
  /// `= false`, so it is not re-sent either.
  bool _rejectIfSessionEnded(
    Object? issuedUnder,
    RequestOptions options,
    RequestInterceptorHandler handler,
  ) {
    if (issuedUnder is! int || _sessionGeneration.isCurrent(issuedUnder)) {
      return false;
    }
    options.extra[RetryInterceptor.retryExtraKey] = false;
    handler.reject(
      DioException(
        requestOptions: options,
        type: DioExceptionType.cancel,
        message: 'The session this request was sent under has ended.',
      ),
      true,
    );
    return true;
  }

  @override
  Future<void> onError(
    DioException err,
    ErrorInterceptorHandler handler,
  ) async {
    // Handle 401 Unauthorized - refresh token or logout
    if (err.response?.statusCode == 401) {
      // Another origin's 401 says nothing about this API's session, and it
      // was never sent this API's token.
      if (!_targetsApiOrigin(err.requestOptions) ||
          _shouldExcludeEndpoint(err.requestOptions)) {
        return super.onError(err, handler);
      }

      // A 401 for a request sent under a session that has since ended - its
      // user signed out, and maybe someone else signed in - says nothing about
      // the session that is current now. Refreshing would rotate the *current*
      // user's credentials and replay the ended session's request as them, and
      // with nothing stored it would log out whoever is signing in. Neither
      // happens: the request just fails.
      if (!_issuedUnderCurrentSession(err.requestOptions)) {
        _reject(handler, err);
        return;
      }

      return await _handle401Error(err, handler);
    }

    super.onError(err, handler);
  }

  /// Whether [options] was sent under the session that is current now.
  ///
  /// [onRequest] stamps the generation on every API-origin request; one with
  /// no stamp never went through it, and is judged by its 401 alone as before.
  bool _issuedUnderCurrentSession(RequestOptions options) {
    final issuedUnder = options.extra[_issuedUnderGenerationKey];
    return issuedUnder is! int || _sessionGeneration.isCurrent(issuedUnder);
  }

  /// Whether [options] goes to the API's own origin - the scheme, host and
  /// port of its base URL.
  ///
  /// Judged on [RequestOptions.uri], the URL dio will actually fetch, not on
  /// the path alone. dio sends an absolute path as is, and it *joins* a
  /// relative one to the base URL as strings rather than resolving it: with a
  /// base URL that has no path, `@evil.example/x` or `.evil.example/x` lands
  /// on another host although the path has no scheme. A URL that cannot be
  /// parsed counts as foreign, so it never carries the token.
  ///
  /// A client with no base URL - the interceptor installed on a bare `Dio` -
  /// has no origin to compare against, so every request on it counts as the
  /// API's.
  static bool _targetsApiOrigin(RequestOptions options) {
    final api = Uri.parse(options.baseUrl);
    if (!api.hasAuthority) return true;
    final Uri target;
    try {
      target = options.uri;
    } on FormatException {
      return false;
    }
    return target.scheme == api.scheme &&
        target.host == api.host &&
        target.port == api.port;
  }

  /// Whether [options] targets one of the auth endpoints themselves, whose
  /// 401 means the credentials were refused rather than that a token expired.
  ///
  /// An exact match on the endpoint path, not `contains`: a route that merely
  /// contains `/auth/login` - `/users/auth/login-history` - is an ordinary
  /// authenticated route and must refresh like any other.
  /// The query string is ignored, and an
  /// absolute URL is reduced to its path below the base URL's.
  bool _shouldExcludeEndpoint(RequestOptions options) {
    return _excludedEndpoints.contains(_endpointPath(options));
  }

  /// The endpoint [options] targets, relative to the base URL, with a leading
  /// slash and no query string or trailing slash.
  static String _endpointPath(RequestOptions options) {
    final target = Uri.parse(options.path);
    var path = target.path;
    if (target.hasScheme) {
      final basePath = Uri.parse(options.baseUrl).path;
      if (basePath.isNotEmpty && path.startsWith(basePath)) {
        path = path.substring(basePath.length);
      }
    }
    if (!path.startsWith('/')) path = '/$path';
    if (path.length > 1 && path.endsWith('/')) {
      path = path.substring(0, path.length - 1);
    }
    return path;
  }

  /// Handles 401 Unauthorized errors by attempting token refresh
  ///
  /// Every exit path completes both this request's handler and every queued
  /// handler: a handler that is neither resolved nor rejected leaves its
  /// caller awaiting a future that never finishes.
  ///
  /// No exit path persists a credential belonging to a session that ended
  /// while the refresh was in flight. See [_sessionGeneration].
  Future<void> _handle401Error(
    DioException err,
    ErrorInterceptorHandler handler,
  ) async {
    // No retry-count check: the replay goes through `retryDio()`, which has
    // no `AuthInterceptor`, so a replay answered 401 cannot re-enter this
    // method - it surfaces from `_retryRequest` below and logs out there.

    // If refresh is already in progress, queue this request
    if (_isRefreshing) {
      return _queueRequest(err, handler);
    }

    // Start token refresh
    _isRefreshing = true;
    AccessTokenSnapshot? refreshedSnapshot;

    // Read before the round trip, checked after it: anything that ends the
    // session in between makes this value stale for good.
    final generation = _sessionGeneration.current;

    try {
      final AccessTokenSnapshot? beforeRefresh;
      try {
        beforeRefresh = await _tokenStore.getAccessTokenSnapshot();
      } on Object catch (error) {
        _reject(handler, _asDioException(err, error));
        return;
      }
      if (!_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation) ||
          beforeRefresh == null ||
          !identical(beforeRefresh.revision, _tokenStore.credentialRevision)) {
        _reject(handler, _selectionChanged(err));
        return;
      }
      final result = await _refreshToken();

      if (!_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation)) {
        // The session this refresh belonged to ended while it was in flight -
        // a logout, a forced logout, or a fresh sign-in. Persist nothing and
        // replay nothing: the teardown already emptied the store, and a newer
        // sign-in may have filled it again with credentials that are not ours
        // to overwrite.
        //
        // Deliberately *not* a _logoutUser() call. Whoever advanced the
        // generation has already torn the session down, and repeating it would
        // clear a session that started after this refresh did - the exact
        // damage the guard exists to prevent, inflicted from the other side.
        //
        // refreshedSnapshot stays null, so the `finally` drain rejects every
        // queued request rather than replaying it with a dead token.
        _reject(handler, err);
        return;
      }

      final newToken = result.isSuccess ? result.dataOrNull : null;

      if (result
          case ResultFailure(
            :final failure,
          )
          when !_endsSession(failure)) {
        // Transport trouble on the refresh endpoint, not a dead refresh token:
        // the session is still good, so nothing is torn down and nothing is
        // replayed. The caller fails with the refresh failure as the cause
        // rather than with this 401, whose "please login again" would be
        // false. refreshedSnapshot stays null,
        // so the `finally` drain rejects every queued request too.
        _reject(handler, _refreshFailedTransiently(err, failure));
        return;
      }

      if (newToken == null) {
        // Refresh failed (or returned nothing usable), logout user
        await _logoutUser();
        _reject(handler, err);
        return;
      }

      final AccessTokenSnapshot? selected;
      try {
        selected = await _selectRefreshResult(
          newToken,
          beforeRefresh,
          generation,
        );
      } on Object catch (error) {
        // The callback succeeded. A selection/persistence failure must not
        // clear credentials another rotation may have installed meanwhile.
        _reject(handler, _asDioException(err, error));
        return;
      }
      if (selected == null) {
        _reject(handler, _selectionChanged(err));
        return;
      }

      // Checked again because selection/persistence may await the platform:
      // a logout or sign-in landing meanwhile makes the replay belong to a
      // session that has ended, and sending it would put the fresh token on
      // the wire after the user signed out.
      // Same exit as the check above, for the same reasons: no replay, no
      // _logoutUser(), and the `finally` drain rejects every queued request.
      if (!_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation)) {
        _reject(handler, err);
        return;
      }
      refreshedSnapshot = selected;

      // Retry original request with new token.
      //
      // Its own `try`, because a failed replay is not a failed refresh. The
      // token is fresh and stored, so a transient replay failure (a 503, a
      // timeout) fails this one request and nothing else: the session stays,
      // and the `finally` drain still replays the queued requests.
      //
      // A 401 ends the session only while the selected credential is still
      // current. An older bearer being refused must not clear a later rotation.
      try {
        // Selection linearizes here; independent rotations after dispatch
        // may still make this request's bearer obsolete.
        if (!_usableCurrentSnapshot(selected)) {
          _reject(handler, _selectionChanged(err));
          return;
        }
        final retryResponse = await _retryRequest(err, selected.token!);
        handler.resolve(retryResponse);
      } on Object catch (e) {
        if (e is DioException &&
            e.response?.statusCode == 401 &&
            _sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation) &&
            _usableCurrentSnapshot(selected)) {
          refreshedSnapshot = null;
          await _logoutUser();
        }
        _reject(handler, _asDioException(err, e));
      }
    }
    // Catches Error as well as Exception on purpose: a TypeError from a
    // malformed refresh response must not leave _isRefreshing stuck true.
    on Object catch (e) {
      refreshedSnapshot = null;
      if (_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation)) await _logoutUser();
      _reject(handler, _asDioException(err, e));
    } finally {
      // Reset before draining: a 401 arriving during the drain must be able
      // to start a fresh refresh rather than queue behind a finished one.
      _isRefreshing = false;
      await _drainPendingRequests(refreshedSnapshot, generation);
    }
  }

  Future<AccessTokenSnapshot?> _selectRefreshResult(
    String candidate,
    AccessTokenSnapshot beforeRefresh,
    int generation,
  ) async {
    final current = await _tokenStore.getAccessTokenSnapshot();
    if (!_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation) ||
        current == null ||
        !identical(current.revision, _tokenStore.credentialRevision)) {
      return null;
    }
    // Repository callbacks persist themselves. A delayed result cannot own
    // a write after any newer credential mutation, even an ABA rotation.
    if (!identical(beforeRefresh.revision, current.revision)) {
      // A refresh-only write by a custom callback is indistinguishable from
      // an ABA rotation here. Neither the old access token nor the returned
      // candidate can safely be associated with the stored refresh token.
      if (current.token == beforeRefresh.token && current.token != candidate) {
        return null;
      }
      return _usableCurrentSnapshot(current) ? current : null;
    }
    if (candidate.isEmpty) return null;
    if (current.token == candidate) return current;
    final outcome = await _tokenStore.setAccessTokenIfCurrent(
      current,
      candidate,
    );
    if (!_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation)) return null;
    if (outcome == AccessTokenWriteResult.failed) {
      throw const CacheException(
        'Could not persist the refreshed access token.',
      );
    }
    if (outcome == AccessTokenWriteResult.conflict) return null;
    final updated = await _tokenStore.getAccessTokenSnapshot();
    return _sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation) &&
            _usableCurrentSnapshot(updated)
        ? updated
        : null;
  }

  bool _usableCurrentSnapshot(AccessTokenSnapshot? snapshot) {
    final token = snapshot?.token;
    return snapshot != null &&
        token != null &&
        token.isNotEmpty &&
        identical(snapshot.revision, _tokenStore.credentialRevision);
  }

  DioException _selectionChanged(DioException original) => DioException(
    requestOptions: original.requestOptions,
    response: original.response,
    type: DioExceptionType.cancel,
    message: 'Credentials changed while selecting the refresh result.',
  );

  /// Fails the request with [error] **without ending the error chain**.
  ///
  /// In dio's error chain, a bare `handler.reject(error)` skips every
  /// interceptor registered after this one - `RetryInterceptor`,
  /// `ApiLoggingInterceptor` and `ErrorInterceptor` in `ApiClient` - so the
  /// 401 would reach the caller unlogged and unmapped. `next` hands it on
  /// (on every dio 5.x, with no flag a later edit could drop), so it is
  /// logged and mapped like any other failing status. Every failure exit in
  /// this class goes through here.
  ///
  /// Forwarded, but **never re-sent**. The error is marked with
  /// [RetryInterceptor.retryExtraKey] `= false` before it moves on, so
  /// `RetryInterceptor` passes it through instead of replaying it. Every exit
  /// that reaches here has already ended the request's session or given up on
  /// it, and a failed replay's options carry the bearer token minted for the
  /// session that was just torn down, which must not go back on the wire.
  void _reject(ErrorInterceptorHandler handler, DioException error) {
    error.requestOptions.extra[RetryInterceptor.retryExtraKey] = false;
    handler.next(error);
  }

  /// Whether a failed refresh means the session is over.
  ///
  /// Fails closed. Only two shapes are known to be transport trouble rather
  /// than a dead credential, and only those leave the session alone:
  ///
  /// - a [NetworkFailure], meaning a timeout, connection error or bad
  ///   certificate on the refresh call;
  /// - an untagged [ServerFailure]. `AuthRepositoryImpl` tags the statuses
  ///   that mean the refresh token is dead (400, 401 and 403) with
  ///   [refreshTokenExpiredCode], so what is left is 408, 429 and 5xx.
  ///
  /// Everything else ends the session:
  ///
  /// - the [refreshTokenExpiredCode] tag, on any failure type;
  /// - core's own `NO_TOKEN_REFRESHER`;
  /// - an untagged auth, unknown or other failure from a custom refresher.
  ///
  /// The last case matters: an unrecognised failure is not given the benefit
  /// of the doubt, because a session that can never refresh would otherwise
  /// stay up indefinitely.
  static bool _endsSession(Failure failure) {
    if (failure.code == refreshTokenExpiredCode) return true;
    return failure is! NetworkFailure && failure is! ServerFailure;
  }

  /// The error a request fails with when its refresh failed transiently.
  ///
  /// `DioExceptionType.unknown` with the failure's message, so
  /// `ErrorInterceptor` maps it to a `NetworkException` that says the session
  /// could not be refreshed. The original 401 would map to a
  /// `ServerException` telling the user to sign in again, when the session is
  /// still good.
  DioException _refreshFailedTransiently(DioException original, Failure f) {
    return DioException(
      requestOptions: original.requestOptions,
      error: f,
      message:
          'Could not refresh the session (${f.code ?? f.runtimeType}): '
          '${f.message}',
    );
  }

  /// Wraps a non-Dio failure so the caller still gets a [DioException].
  DioException _asDioException(DioException original, Object error) {
    if (error is DioException) {
      return error;
    }
    return DioException(
      requestOptions: original.requestOptions,
      error: error,
      response: original.response,
      type: original.type,
    );
  }

  /// Retries the original request with a new token
  ///
  /// The body goes through [RequestBodyReplay]: a [FormData] is sent as a
  /// clone, because dio finalizes the original on the first send, and a body
  /// that cannot be read twice fails the replay with a [DioException] naming
  /// the reason.
  Future<Response<dynamic>> _retryRequest(
    DioException err,
    String newToken,
  ) async {
    final requestOptions = err.requestOptions;

    if (!RequestBodyReplay.canReplay(requestOptions.data)) {
      final reason = RequestBodyReplay.reason(requestOptions.data);
      throw DioException(
        requestOptions: requestOptions,
        message: reason,
        error: NetworkException(
          reason,
          code: RequestBodyReplay.notReplayableCode,
        ),
      );
    }

    // Create new request options with updated headers
    final newOptions = requestOptions.copyWith(
      headers: {
        ...requestOptions.headers,
        'Authorization': 'Bearer $newToken',
      },
    );

    // Replay through the shared retry client so the transport - and its
    // certificate pinning - matches the original request.
    return await retryDio().request(
      newOptions.path,
      data: RequestBodyReplay.bodyFor(newOptions.data),
      queryParameters: newOptions.queryParameters,
      // Cancelling the original request must abort the replay too.
      cancelToken: requestOptions.cancelToken,
      onSendProgress: requestOptions.onSendProgress,
      onReceiveProgress: requestOptions.onReceiveProgress,
      options: Options(
        method: newOptions.method,
        headers: newOptions.headers,
        contentType: newOptions.contentType,
        responseType: newOptions.responseType,
        followRedirects: newOptions.followRedirects,
        maxRedirects: newOptions.maxRedirects,
        validateStatus: newOptions.validateStatus,
        receiveTimeout: newOptions.receiveTimeout,
        sendTimeout: newOptions.sendTimeout,
        transformTimeout: newOptions.transformTimeout,
        // The original's per-request metadata travels with the replay - its
        // performance trace among it, which the replay client's borrowed
        // PerformanceInterceptor finishes with the replay's outcome.
        extra: Map<String, dynamic>.of(newOptions.extra),
      ),
    );
  }

  /// Queues a request to be retried after token refresh completes
  void _queueRequest(DioException err, ErrorInterceptorHandler handler) {
    _pendingRequests.add(_PendingRequest(err, handler));
  }

  /// Completes every queued request.
  ///
  /// After a successful selection, each request selects a fresh certified
  /// token before replay. Without one the refresh failed, so each handler is
  /// rejected with its own original error - never left dangling.
  ///
  /// [generation] is the session the refresh was started under. A queued
  /// request is **rejected, not replayed**, once that generation is no longer
  /// current: replaying would put `Authorization: Bearer ...` on the wire for
  /// a user who has signed out, and the caller of a request issued before the
  /// logout has no use for its body afterwards. Rejection still completes the
  /// handler, so nothing is stranded.
  ///
  /// The check is inside the loop rather than above it because each replay is
  /// awaited: a logout landing part-way through the drain must stop the
  /// remaining replays too, not only the ones not yet started.
  Future<void> _drainPendingRequests(
    AccessTokenSnapshot? refreshedSnapshot,
    int generation,
  ) async {
    if (_pendingRequests.isEmpty) {
      return;
    }

    final requests = List<_PendingRequest>.from(_pendingRequests);
    _pendingRequests.clear();

    for (final pending in requests) {
      // The last check covers a request that queued under an older session
      // than the refresh's, which onError already turns away; kept here so the
      // drain never replays one as the newer user whatever path queued it.
      if (refreshedSnapshot == null ||
          !_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation) ||
          !_issuedUnderCurrentSession(pending.error.requestOptions)) {
        _reject(pending.handler, pending.error);
        continue;
      }

      try {
        final current = await _tokenStore.getAccessTokenSnapshot();
        if (!_sessionGeneration.isCurrent(generation) ||
            !_usableCurrentSnapshot(current)) {
          _reject(pending.handler, _selectionChanged(pending.error));
          continue;
        }
        final retryResponse = await _retryRequest(
          pending.error,
          current!.token!,
        );
        pending.handler.resolve(retryResponse);
      } on Object catch (e) {
        // If retry fails, reject the pending request
        _reject(pending.handler, _asDioException(pending.error, e));
      }
    }
  }

  /// Logs out the user by clearing all authentication data.
  ///
  /// Clears the tokens, the cached user blob and the HTTP response cache, so
  /// this forced logout leaves exactly the state `AuthRepository.logout()`
  /// leaves: a leftover blob would read as a session with no token, and
  /// leftover cached bodies would outlive the session.
  ///
  /// The last step tells the **running app**, which renders the in-memory
  /// session, *after* storage is emptied, so any listener reacting to the
  /// change (the router guard, a screen refetching) already sees a device
  /// with no session on it.
  ///
  /// Each step is guarded separately and no step is reachable only through
  /// another: cleanup must never stop a handler from being completed, and a
  /// failure - or absence - of one step must not skip the rest.
  ///
  /// The teardown is registered with the [SessionGeneration] before anything
  /// else, so a sign-in - even one a session listener starts - waits for it
  /// rather than storing credentials these steps would then delete. The
  /// generation is then advanced, synchronously, ahead of every `await` in
  /// [_tearDownSession], so anything still in flight for the ending session -
  /// a refresh, a token write - is already stale by the time it reaches its
  /// own check, rather than racing the teardown it is about to undo.
  Future<void> _logoutUser() {
    final done = Completer<void>();
    _sessionGeneration.trackTeardown(done.future);
    final teardown = _tearDownSession();
    done.complete(teardown);
    return teardown;
  }

  Future<void> _tearDownSession() async {
    // Nothing in flight may write a credential for the session being ended.
    // Unguarded on purpose: it is a synchronous integer increment that cannot
    // throw, and a try/catch here would suggest it might.
    _sessionGeneration.invalidate();

    // Clear tokens from secure storage
    try {
      await _tokenStore.clearAllTokens();
    } on Object catch (_) {
      // Never let cleanup failure stop a handler from being completed.
    }

    // Clear the cached user from non-sensitive storage
    final keyValueStore = _keyValueStore;
    if (keyValueStore != null) {
      try {
        await keyValueStore.remove(AppConstants.userDataKey);
      } on Object catch (_) {
        // Same reason as above: cleanup is best-effort, completion is not.
      }
    }

    // Drop cached response bodies: they are local session state too.
    //
    // Guarded rather than early-returned, so a step added after this one is
    // never skipped.
    final responseCache = _responseCache;
    if (responseCache != null) {
      try {
        await responseCache.clearCache();
      } on Object catch (_) {
        // Same reason again. A cache that cannot be emptied must not strand
        // the 401 that triggered this logout.
      }
    }

    // Tell the running app its session is over, so the UI stops presenting it.
    final sessionSink = _sessionSink;
    if (sessionSink != null) {
      try {
        sessionSink.onSessionTerminated();
      } on Object catch (_) {
        // Deliberately swallowed, and load-bearing: a background request can
        // 401 after the app's ProviderContainer is gone, and resolving a
        // disposed provider throws. There is no in-memory session left to
        // clear in that case, and the request's handler must still complete.
      }
    }
  }
}
import 'package:dio/dio.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/errors/dio_exception_mapper.dart';

/// Interceptor for converting DioException to domain exceptions
///
/// This interceptor must be added LAST in the interceptor chain. Its
/// [onError] terminates the chain with `handler.reject(...)`, and dio runs
/// error handlers in registration order, so any interceptor registered after
/// it never sees the error - that would disable retry, 401 token refresh,
/// performance trace teardown and error logging.
class ErrorInterceptor extends Interceptor {
  @override
  void onError(DioException err, ErrorInterceptorHandler handler) {
    // Convert DioException to domain exception
    final domainException = DioExceptionMapper.map(err);

    // Reject with a DioException that contains the domain exception
    // This allows the domain exception to be extracted later in catch blocks
    handler.reject(
      DioException(
        requestOptions: err.requestOptions,
        error: domainException,
        type: err.type,
        response: err.response,
        message: domainException.message,
      ),
    );
  }
}
import 'dart:async';

import 'package:dio/dio.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/contracts/network_contracts.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/errors/exceptions.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/request_cancellation.dart';

/// Dio adapter for [INetworkClient].
class DioNetworkClient implements INetworkClient {
  /// Creates a transport adapter over a configured Dio instance.
  ///
  /// A positive [deadline] bounds each [send] end to end: the whole
  /// interceptor chain, including retry backoff and a 401 refresh-and-replay,
  /// must finish within it. Dio's connect, send and receive timeouts each
  /// bound one phase of one attempt, so without this a request that keeps
  /// retrying has no upper bound at all. `ApiClient` passes
  /// `AppConfig.apiTimeout` (`API_TIMEOUT`). Null, zero and negative values
  /// disable the total deadline; per-attempt and caller cancellation remain.
  DioNetworkClient(this._dio, {Duration? deadline}) : _deadline = deadline;

  final Dio _dio;
  final Duration? _deadline;

  /// Sends [request]; [cancellation], when given, cancels it on demand.
  ///
  /// The [INetworkClient] contract has no cancellation parameter, so it is an
  /// optional extra here, used by `ApiClient`.
  /// One dio [CancelToken] backs both the caller's cancellation and the
  /// deadline, and every re-send of the request - a retry, a 401 replay -
  /// carries it.
  @override
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> send(
    NetworkRequest request, {
    RequestCancellation? cancellation,
  }) async {
    final deadline = _deadline;
    final cancelToken = CancelToken();
    if (cancellation != null) {
      unawaited(
        cancellation.whenCancelled.then((reason) {
          if (!cancelToken.isCancelled) {
            cancelToken.cancel(reason ?? 'Cancelled by the caller');
          }
        }),
      );
    }
    final pending = _send(request, cancelToken);
    if (deadline == null || deadline <= Duration.zero) return await pending;
    return await pending.timeout(
      deadline,
      onTimeout: () {
        // Stop the work as well as the wait; the abandoned future's own
        // cancellation error has no listener left to reach.
        cancelToken.cancel('API_TIMEOUT deadline exceeded');
        unawaited(pending.then<void>((_) {}, onError: (Object _) {}));
        // RECEIVE_TIMEOUT so the failure localizes as a timeout: the client
        // gave up waiting for a response.
        throw NetworkException(
          'Request deadline exceeded: no response within '
          '${deadline.inSeconds}s (API_TIMEOUT)',
          code: 'RECEIVE_TIMEOUT',
        );
      },
    );
  }

  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> _send(
    NetworkRequest request,
    CancelToken cancelToken,
  ) async {
    try {
      final response = await _dio.request<dynamic>(
        request.path,
        data: request.body,
        queryParameters: request.queryParameters,
        cancelToken: cancelToken,
        options: Options(
          method: _toHttpMethod(request.method),
          headers: request.headers,
          sendTimeout: request.timeout,
          receiveTimeout: request.timeout,
        ),
      );

      return NetworkResponse<dynamic>(
        statusCode: response.statusCode ?? 0,
        data: response.data,
        headers: response.headers.map,
      );
    } on DioException catch (e) {
      final appError = e.error;
      if (appError is Exception) {
        throw appError;
      }
      throw NetworkError(
        message: e.message ?? 'Network request failed',
        code: e.type.name,
        statusCode: e.response?.statusCode,
        cause: e,
      );
    }
  }

  String _toHttpMethod(NetworkMethod method) {
    switch (method) {
      case NetworkMethod.get:
        return 'GET';
      case NetworkMethod.post:
        return 'POST';
      case NetworkMethod.put:
        return 'PUT';
      case NetworkMethod.patch:
        return 'PATCH';
      case NetworkMethod.delete:
        return 'DELETE';
    }
  }
}
import 'dart:async';

/// A handle that cancels an `ApiClient` request, with no dio type in sight.
///
/// Pass one to any `ApiClient` verb and call [cancel] when the result is no
/// longer wanted - a screen disposed, a search superseded by the next
/// keystroke. The caller's future then fails with a `NetworkException` coded
/// `REQUEST_CANCELLED`, and nothing further reaches the transport for that
/// request: not a `RetryInterceptor` re-send, not a 401 refresh's replay.
///
/// One handle may cover several requests; cancelling it cancels them all.
/// Cancelling twice, or after the requests finished, does nothing.
class RequestCancellation {
  final Completer<String?> _cancelled = Completer<String?>();

  /// Whether [cancel] has been called.
  bool get isCancelled => _cancelled.isCompleted;

  /// Completes with the reason given to [cancel], once it is called.
  Future<String?> get whenCancelled => _cancelled.future;

  /// Cancels every request this handle was passed to.
  void cancel([String? reason]) {
    if (!_cancelled.isCompleted) _cancelled.complete(reason);
  }
}
// Lightweight boundary contract file; repetitive per-member docs are omitted.
// ignore_for_file: public_member_api_docs

/// Supported HTTP methods for transport-agnostic requests.
enum NetworkMethod { get, post, put, patch, delete }

/// Generic network request contract.
class NetworkRequest {
  const NetworkRequest({
    required this.path,
    required this.method,
    this.body,
    this.headers = const <String, String>{},
    this.queryParameters = const <String, dynamic>{},
    this.timeout,
  });

  final String path;
  final NetworkMethod method;
  final dynamic body;
  final Map<String, String> headers;
  final Map<String, dynamic> queryParameters;
  final Duration? timeout;
}

/// Generic network response contract.
class NetworkResponse<T> {
  const NetworkResponse({
    required this.statusCode,
    required this.data,
    this.headers = const <String, List<String>>{},
  });

  final int statusCode;
  final T data;
  final Map<String, List<String>> headers;
}

/// Normalized network error contract at the transport boundary.
class NetworkError implements Exception {
  const NetworkError({
    required this.message,
    this.code,
    this.statusCode,
    this.cause,
  });

  final String message;
  final String? code;
  final int? statusCode;
  final Object? cause;

  /// Carries the message, code and status, so wrapping this in another
  /// exception never degrades to `Instance of 'NetworkError'`.
  @override
  String toString() {
    final details = <String>[
      if (code != null) 'code: $code',
      if (statusCode != null) 'statusCode: $statusCode',
    ];
    final label = details.isEmpty
        ? 'NetworkError'
        : 'NetworkError(${details.join(', ')})';
    return '$label: $message';
  }
}

/// Transport-agnostic network client contract.
abstract class INetworkClient {
  Future<NetworkResponse<dynamic>> send(NetworkRequest request);
}

/// Locally persisted HTTP response cache, as seen from outside the transport.
///
/// Exists so session teardown can drop cached response bodies without the auth
/// layer importing `ApiClient` or any dio type.
abstract class IHttpResponseCache {
  Future<void> clearCache();
}
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/config/env_config.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/api_logging_interceptor.dart';

/// Application configuration with environment-aware defaults
///
/// This class provides typed access to configuration values with:
/// - Environment-aware defaults
/// - Feature flags
/// - Network timeout configuration
/// - Debug utilities
///
/// Usage:
/// ```dart
/// final baseUrl = AppConfig.baseUrl;
/// if (AppConfig.enableLogging) {
///   logger.info('App started');
/// }
/// ```
class AppConfig {
  AppConfig._();

  // ==========================================================================
  // Environment Detection
  // ==========================================================================

  /// The environment names the rest of [AppConfig] recognises.
  ///
  /// Adding an environment means adding it here and to every switch on
  /// [environment] (`baseUrl`, the `is*` getters); until then it is reported
  /// by [environmentMisconfigured].
  static const List<String> knownEnvironments = [
    'development',
    'staging',
    'production',
  ];

  /// Current environment name (development, staging, production)
  ///
  /// Trimmed and lower-cased, so `Production ` is `production`.
  ///
  /// Can be set via:
  /// - .env file: `ENVIRONMENT=production`
  /// - --dart-define: `--dart-define=ENVIRONMENT=production`
  /// - Default: `development`
  static String get environment => EnvConfig.get(
    'ENVIRONMENT',
    defaultValue: 'development',
  ).trim().toLowerCase();

  /// Whether [environment] is none of [knownEnvironments].
  ///
  /// This is a misconfiguration, and a dangerous one: [isDevelopment],
  /// [isStaging] and [isProduction] are then all false, so [baseUrl] falls
  /// back to the development `http://localhost:3000` and [enableSslPinning]
  /// is off. `ENVIRONMENT=prod` used to do exactly that with no warning.
  /// [printConfig] reports it, and it is in
  /// [getDebugInfo].
  static bool get environmentMisconfigured =>
      !knownEnvironments.contains(environment);

  /// Returns true if the current environment is development
  static bool get isDevelopment => environment == 'development';

  /// Returns true if the current environment is production
  static bool get isProduction => environment == 'production';

  /// Returns true if the current environment is staging
  static bool get isStaging => environment == 'staging';

  /// Returns true if the app is running in debug mode
  static bool get isDebugMode => kDebugMode;

  /// Returns true if the app is running in release mode
  static bool get isReleaseMode => kReleaseMode;

  // ==========================================================================
  // API Configuration
  // ==========================================================================

  /// Base URL for API requests
  ///
  /// Environment-aware defaults:
  /// - Development: `http://localhost:3000`, which only a profile or release
  ///   build ever contacts: without `BASE_URL` a development debug build has
  ///   no backend ([hasBackend]) and signs in offline instead
  /// - Staging: `https://api-staging.example.com`
  /// - Production: `https://api.example.com`
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via:
  /// - .env file: `BASE_URL=https://api.example.com`
  /// - --dart-define: `--dart-define=BASE_URL=https://api.example.com`
  static String get baseUrl {
    final envValue = EnvConfig.get('BASE_URL');
    if (envValue.isNotEmpty) {
      return envValue;
    }

    // Environment-aware defaults
    switch (environment) {
      case 'production':
        return 'https://api.example.com';
      case 'staging':
        return 'https://api-staging.example.com';
      case 'development':
      default:
        return 'http://localhost:3000';
    }
  }

  /// Whether this build talks to a backend: `BASE_URL` is set, or
  /// [environment] is not `development`.
  ///
  /// This is the one rule for "is there a backend". Where it is false in a
  /// debug build, auth - the only feature that calls the network - signs in
  /// with the offline demo account, so the app as a whole runs without a
  /// server and nothing contacts the development [baseUrl] fallback. To use
  /// a local server in development, set `BASE_URL` to it.
  static bool get hasBackend => hasBackendFor(
    environment: environment,
    baseUrl: EnvConfig.get('BASE_URL'),
  );

  /// The rule behind [hasBackend], with every input explicit.
  static bool hasBackendFor({
    required String environment,
    required String baseUrl,
  }) => baseUrl.trim().isNotEmpty || environment != 'development';

  /// API timeout in seconds
  ///
  /// Default: 30 seconds
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `API_TIMEOUT=60`
  static int get apiTimeout =>
      EnvConfig.getInt('API_TIMEOUT', defaultValue: 30);

  /// API connect timeout in seconds
  ///
  /// Default: 10 seconds
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `API_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=15`
  static int get apiConnectTimeout =>
      EnvConfig.getInt('API_CONNECT_TIMEOUT', defaultValue: 10);

  /// API receive timeout in seconds
  ///
  /// Default: 30 seconds
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `API_RECEIVE_TIMEOUT=60`
  static int get apiReceiveTimeout =>
      EnvConfig.getInt('API_RECEIVE_TIMEOUT', defaultValue: 30);

  /// API send timeout in seconds
  ///
  /// Default: 30 seconds
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `API_SEND_TIMEOUT=60`
  static int get apiSendTimeout =>
      EnvConfig.getInt('API_SEND_TIMEOUT', defaultValue: 30);

  /// List of valid SSL certificate fingerprints (SHA-256)
  ///
  /// Can be configured via .env (comma separated):
  /// `API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS=f4:34:b2...,c8:2f:13...`
  static List<String> get apiSslFingerprints {
    final envValue = EnvConfig.get('API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS');
    if (envValue.isNotEmpty) {
      return envValue
          .split(',')
          .map((s) => s.trim().replaceAll(':', '').toLowerCase())
          .where((s) => s.isNotEmpty)
          .toList();
    }
    return const [];
  }

  /// Enable SSL/Certificate Pinning
  ///
  /// Enforces verification of server certificate fingerprints using
  /// [apiSslFingerprints].
  /// Default behavior:
  /// - Staging/Production: `true`
  /// - Development: `true` once [apiSslFingerprints] holds any, `false`
  ///   otherwise (to allow proxy debugging like Charles/Proxyman). Configured
  ///   fingerprints are a statement that this backend is pinned; ignoring them
  ///   because the environment says development shipped releases unpinned.
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `ENABLE_SSL_PINNING=false`. Only the
  /// spellings in [sslPinningOnValues] and [sslPinningOffValues] (any case,
  /// trimmed) override the default. Anything else - a typo such as `ture`,
  /// `enabled`, or whitespace - keeps the default above rather than turning
  /// pinning off, and [sslPinningFlagUnrecognised] reports it; a release
  /// refuses to start on it.
  static bool get enableSslPinning {
    final flag = EnvConfig.get('ENABLE_SSL_PINNING').trim().toLowerCase();
    if (sslPinningOnValues.contains(flag)) return true;
    if (sslPinningOffValues.contains(flag)) return false;
    return isStaging || isProduction || apiSslFingerprints.isNotEmpty;
  }

  /// `ENABLE_SSL_PINNING` values that turn pinning on.
  static const Set<String> sslPinningOnValues = {'true', '1', 'yes', 'on'};

  /// `ENABLE_SSL_PINNING` values that turn pinning off.
  static const Set<String> sslPinningOffValues = {'false', '0', 'no', 'off'};

  /// Whether `ENABLE_SSL_PINNING` is set to something that is neither an on
  /// nor an off spelling.
  ///
  /// It used to be read as false - any value but `true`, `1`, `yes` or `on`
  /// - so a typo silently shipped a staging or production release unpinned.
  /// [enableSslPinning] now ignores such a value, and `main()` refuses to
  /// start a release that carries one.
  static bool get sslPinningFlagUnrecognised {
    final raw = EnvConfig.get('ENABLE_SSL_PINNING');
    if (raw.isEmpty) return false;
    final flag = raw.trim().toLowerCase();
    return !sslPinningOnValues.contains(flag) &&
        !sslPinningOffValues.contains(flag);
  }

  /// Whether a release build is configured as `development`.
  ///
  /// `development` is the default and the value `.env.example` ships, so a
  /// release built from an unedited `.env` lands here: debug routes
  /// registered, HTTP logging on, pinning off unless fingerprints are set.
  /// `main()` refuses to start on it. Profile builds are allowed - profiling
  /// against a development backend is what `--profile` is for.
  static bool releaseBuiltAsDevelopment({bool releaseMode = kReleaseMode}) =>
      releaseMode && isDevelopment;

  /// Whether certificate pinning is actually enforced.
  ///
  /// [enableSslPinning] is only the request. With no entries in
  /// [apiSslFingerprints] there is nothing to pin against, so the transport
  /// falls back to the system trust store and pinning is *not* in effect.
  ///
  /// Never in effect on Flutter web, where the browser owns TLS and
  /// `SslPinning` installs no adapter. This
  /// mirrors `SslPinning.isEffective`, so `getDebugInfo` cannot report a web
  /// build as pinned once `--dart-define` reaches web.
  static bool get sslPinningEffective =>
      !kIsWeb && enableSslPinning && apiSslFingerprints.isNotEmpty;

  /// Whether pinning is requested but unenforceable (no fingerprints).
  ///
  /// This is a misconfiguration: the app believes it is pinned and is not.
  ///
  /// Never on web either: pinning does not apply there, so an empty list
  /// misconfigures nothing.
  static bool get sslPinningMisconfigured =>
      !kIsWeb && enableSslPinning && apiSslFingerprints.isEmpty;

  /// Whether a staging or production release talks to a non-`https`
  /// [baseUrl].
  ///
  /// Android and iOS block cleartext by default, but web, Windows, Linux and
  /// macOS do not: an `http` BASE_URL sent bearer and refresh tokens in
  /// cleartext, and pinning means nothing without TLS. `main()` refuses to
  /// start on it. Development and debug or profile builds are not affected.
  static bool releaseWithoutHttps({bool releaseMode = kReleaseMode}) =>
      releaseMode &&
      (isStaging || isProduction) &&
      Uri.tryParse(baseUrl)?.scheme.toLowerCase() != 'https';

  /// Whether a release build requests pinning it cannot enforce.
  ///
  /// [sslPinningMisconfigured] in a release build: the assertion that flags it
  /// in debug is stripped, so the app would otherwise talk to the API through
  /// the system trust store while every default says it is pinned. `main()`
  /// refuses to start on it. Setting `ENABLE_SSL_PINNING=false` explicitly is
  /// the opt-out. Profile builds are allowed, as for
  /// [releaseBuiltAsDevelopment].
  static bool releasePinningMisconfigured({bool releaseMode = kReleaseMode}) =>
      releaseMode && sslPinningMisconfigured;

  // ==========================================================================
  // Feature Flags
  // ==========================================================================

  /// Enable general application logging
  ///
  /// This flag controls general application logging (e.g., debug messages,
  /// info logs, error logs). Use this for application-level logging.
  ///
  /// Default: `true` in every environment.
  ///
  /// Production used to default to `false`, which turned `LoggingService`
  /// into a no-op and dropped the info-and-above JSON lines its file output
  /// exists to keep. What each environment
  /// logs, and where, is decided by `LoggingService`, not by this switch.
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env or a dart-define: `ENABLE_LOGGING=false`
  /// turns every output off.
  ///
  /// See also: [enableHttpLogging] for HTTP request/response logging
  static bool get enableLogging {
    if (EnvConfig.has('ENABLE_LOGGING')) {
      return EnvConfig.getBool('ENABLE_LOGGING');
    }
    return true;
  }

  /// Enable analytics
  ///
  /// Default behavior:
  /// - Development: `false`
  /// - Staging: `true`
  /// - Production: `true`
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `ENABLE_ANALYTICS=true`
  static bool get enableAnalytics {
    if (EnvConfig.has('ENABLE_ANALYTICS')) {
      return EnvConfig.getBool('ENABLE_ANALYTICS');
    }
    return isProduction || isStaging;
  }

  /// Enable crash reporting
  ///
  /// Default behavior:
  /// - Development: `false`
  /// - Staging: `true`
  /// - Production: `true`
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `ENABLE_CRASH_REPORTING=true`
  static bool get enableCrashReporting {
    if (EnvConfig.has('ENABLE_CRASH_REPORTING')) {
      return EnvConfig.getBool('ENABLE_CRASH_REPORTING');
    }
    return isProduction || isStaging;
  }

  /// Enable performance monitoring
  ///
  /// Default behavior:
  /// - Development: `false`
  /// - Staging: `true`
  /// - Production: `true`
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `ENABLE_PERFORMANCE_MONITORING=true`
  static bool get enablePerformanceMonitoring {
    if (EnvConfig.has('ENABLE_PERFORMANCE_MONITORING')) {
      return EnvConfig.getBool('ENABLE_PERFORMANCE_MONITORING');
    }
    return isProduction || isStaging;
  }

  /// Enable debug features (e.g., debug menu, verbose logging)
  ///
  /// Default: `true` in development, `false` otherwise
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `ENABLE_DEBUG_FEATURES=false`
  static bool get enableDebugFeatures {
    if (EnvConfig.has('ENABLE_DEBUG_FEATURES')) {
      return EnvConfig.getBool('ENABLE_DEBUG_FEATURES');
    }
    return isDevelopment;
  }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Additional Configuration
  // ==========================================================================

  // No app version or build number here: they are not configuration. They
  // come from pubspec.yaml's `version:`, which the build stamps into the
  // platform bundle; read them from the platform at runtime. The
  // APP_VERSION / APP_BUILD_NUMBER keys this class used to parse were read
  // by nothing.

  /// Enable HTTP request/response logging
  ///
  /// This flag controls HTTP request/response logging specifically for
  /// network operations (e.g., API calls, request/response bodies, headers).
  /// Use this for debugging network issues.
  ///
  /// Default: `true` in development, `false` otherwise
  ///
  /// Can be overridden via .env: `ENABLE_HTTP_LOGGING=false`
  ///
  /// Note: This is separate from [enableLogging]. You can enable HTTP
  /// logging without enabling general application logging, or vice versa.
  static bool get enableHttpLogging {
    if (EnvConfig.has('ENABLE_HTTP_LOGGING')) {
      return EnvConfig.getBool('ENABLE_HTTP_LOGGING');
    }
    return isDevelopment;
  }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Debug Utilities
  // ==========================================================================

  /// Print current configuration to console
  ///
  /// Useful for debugging and verifying configuration values
  static void printConfig() {
    if (kDebugMode) {
      debugPrint('═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════');
      debugPrint('📱 App Configuration');
      debugPrint('═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════');
      debugPrint('Environment: $environment');
      if (environmentMisconfigured) {
        debugPrint(
          '  !! ENVIRONMENT MISCONFIGURED: "$environment" is not one of '
          '${knownEnvironments.join(', ')} - the base URL falls back to '
          'localhost and SSL pinning is off',
        );
      }
      debugPrint('Debug Mode: $isDebugMode');
      debugPrint('Release Mode: $isReleaseMode');
      debugPrint('');
      debugPrint('🌐 API Configuration:');
      // Through redactUrl, as the startup refusal shows it: a BASE_URL can
      // carry userinfo or a token in its query.
      debugPrint('  Base URL: ${ApiLoggingInterceptor.redactUrl(baseUrl)}');
      debugPrint('  Timeout: ${apiTimeout}s');
      debugPrint('  Connect Timeout: ${apiConnectTimeout}s');
      debugPrint('  Receive Timeout: ${apiReceiveTimeout}s');
      debugPrint('  Send Timeout: ${apiSendTimeout}s');
      debugPrint('  SSL Pinning Requested: $enableSslPinning');
      debugPrint('  SSL Pinning Effective: $sslPinningEffective');
      debugPrint('  SSL Fingerprints Configured: ${apiSslFingerprints.length}');
      if (sslPinningFlagUnrecognised) {
        debugPrint(
          '  !! ENABLE_SSL_PINNING is not one of '
          '${sslPinningOnValues.join('/')} or '
          '${sslPinningOffValues.join('/')} - the default applies, and a '
          'release build refuses to start',
        );
      }
      if (sslPinningMisconfigured) {
        debugPrint(
          '  !! SSL PINNING MISCONFIGURED: requested but no fingerprints '
          'configured - nothing is pinned',
        );
      }
      debugPrint('');
      debugPrint('🚩 Feature Flags:');
      debugPrint('  Logging: $enableLogging');
      debugPrint('  Analytics: $enableAnalytics');
      debugPrint('  Crash Reporting: $enableCrashReporting');
      debugPrint('  Performance Monitoring: $enablePerformanceMonitoring');
      debugPrint('  Debug Features: $enableDebugFeatures');
      debugPrint('  HTTP Logging: $enableHttpLogging');
      debugPrint('═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════');
    }
  }

  /// Get configuration as a map for debugging
  ///
  /// Returns a map containing all configuration values. `baseUrl` goes
  /// through [ApiLoggingInterceptor.redactUrl], as in [printConfig]: the map
  /// ends up in logs and bug reports, and a BASE_URL can carry userinfo or a
  /// secret query value.
  static Map<String, dynamic> getDebugInfo() {
    return {
      'environment': environment,
      'environmentMisconfigured': environmentMisconfigured,
      'isDevelopment': isDevelopment,
      'isStaging': isStaging,
      'isProduction': isProduction,
      'isDebugMode': isDebugMode,
      'isReleaseMode': isReleaseMode,
      'baseUrl': ApiLoggingInterceptor.redactUrl(baseUrl),
      'apiTimeout': apiTimeout,
      'apiConnectTimeout': apiConnectTimeout,
      'apiReceiveTimeout': apiReceiveTimeout,
      'apiSendTimeout': apiSendTimeout,
      // Requested vs actually enforced - an empty fingerprint list pins
      // nothing even when enableSslPinning is true.
      'enableSslPinning': enableSslPinning,
      'sslPinningEffective': sslPinningEffective,
      'sslPinningMisconfigured': sslPinningMisconfigured,
      'sslPinningFlagUnrecognised': sslPinningFlagUnrecognised,
      'apiSslFingerprintsCount': apiSslFingerprints.length,
      'enableLogging': enableLogging,
      'enableAnalytics': enableAnalytics,
      'enableCrashReporting': enableCrashReporting,
      'enablePerformanceMonitoring': enablePerformanceMonitoring,
      'enableDebugFeatures': enableDebugFeatures,
      'enableHttpLogging': enableHttpLogging,
      'envConfigInitialized': EnvConfig.isInitialized,
    };
  }
}
import 'package:flutter_dotenv/flutter_dotenv.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/config/dart_defines.dart';

/// Environment configuration loader with fallback chain:
/// 1. A loaded .env file - see [load]; normally there is none
/// 2. --dart-define flags, on every platform including Flutter web, for the
///    keys listed in [DartDefines.values]. This is how `.env` reaches a build:
///    `flutter run --dart-define-from-file=.env`, with nothing bundled
/// 3. Default values
///
/// Dart-define reads go through the compile-time [DartDefines] table rather
/// than `String.fromEnvironment(key)`. A runtime-keyed `String.fromEnvironment`
/// call is not const and therefore always returns `''` in AOT (release and
/// profile) builds, and dart2js does not support it at all. The const table
/// is resolved by every compiler, dart2js included, which is why web needs no
/// special case.
///
/// Usage:
/// ```dart
/// await EnvConfig.load();
/// final value = EnvConfig.get('BASE_URL');
/// ```
class EnvConfig {
  EnvConfig._();

  static bool _isInitialized = false;

  /// Load environment variables from .env file
  ///
  /// This should be called before runApp() in main.dart
  ///
  /// The .env file is optional. If it doesn't exist, the system will fall back
  /// to --dart-define flags or default values.
  ///
  /// [load] reads from the asset bundle, so it only finds a file that is
  /// bundled into the app - and a bundled `.env` ships, readable, inside every
  /// release build and over HTTP on web, which `tool/check_env_assets.dart`
  /// refuses. Pass `.env` as a build input instead:
  /// `--dart-define-from-file=.env`, the second step of the fallback chain
  /// on [EnvConfig]. See "Never ship a secret in the bundle" in
  /// `docs/guides/configuration.md`.
  ///
  /// Example:
  /// ```dart
  /// void main() async {
  ///   WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  ///   await EnvConfig.load();
  ///   runApp(MyApp());
  /// }
  /// ```
  static Future<void> load({String fileName = '.env'}) async {
    try {
      await dotenv.load(fileName: fileName);
      _isInitialized = true;
    } on Exception catch (_) {
      // .env file is optional - fallback to --dart-define or defaults
      // This is expected in CI/CD environments where .env files aren't used
      _isInitialized = false;
    } on Object catch (_) {
      // Catch all other errors (FileNotFoundError, etc.)
      // .env file is optional - fallback to --dart-define or defaults
      _isInitialized = false;
    }
  }

  /// Get environment variable with fallback chain:
  /// 1. .env file value (if loaded)
  /// 2. --dart-define flag value
  /// 3. Default value (if provided)
  ///
  /// Parameters:
  /// - [key]: The environment variable key
  /// - [defaultValue]: Optional default value if not found in .env or
  ///   --dart-define
  ///
  /// Returns:
  /// The environment variable value, or [defaultValue] if not found
  ///
  /// Example:
  /// ```dart
  /// final baseUrl = EnvConfig.get(
  ///   'BASE_URL',
  ///   defaultValue: 'https://api.example.com',
  /// );
  /// ```
  static String get(String key, {String defaultValue = ''}) {
    // Priority 1: Check .env file (if loaded)
    // Use dotenv.get() with fallback for better null safety support
    if (_isInitialized) {
      try {
        final value = dotenv.get(key, fallback: '');
        if (value.isNotEmpty) {
          return value;
        }
      } on Exception {
        // Variable not found in .env, continue to next priority
      }
    }

    // Priority 2: Check --dart-define flags
    final dartDefineValue = DartDefines.lookup(key);
    if (dartDefineValue.isNotEmpty) {
      return dartDefineValue;
    }

    // Priority 3: Return default value
    return defaultValue;
  }

  /// Get environment variable as boolean
  ///
  /// Follows the same priority order as [get]: `.env`, then
  /// `--dart-define`, then [defaultValue] when neither has the key.
  ///
  /// Returns true for: 'true', '1', 'yes', 'on' (case-insensitive, trimmed),
  /// and false for any other value that is present.
  ///
  /// The typed getters used to ask dotenv with `fallback: defaultValue`,
  /// which returns the fallback for a missing key instead of throwing, so a
  /// `.env` without the key hid its `--dart-define`; dotenv also rejected
  /// `yes` and `on`. They now parse the
  /// string [get] resolves.
  static bool getBool(String key, {bool defaultValue = false}) {
    final value = get(key).trim().toLowerCase();
    if (value.isEmpty) return defaultValue;
    return const {'true', '1', 'yes', 'on'}.contains(value);
  }

  /// Get environment variable as integer
  ///
  /// Same priority order as [get]. Returns [defaultValue] when neither source
  /// has the key or the value is not an integer.
  static int getInt(String key, {int defaultValue = 0}) =>
      int.tryParse(get(key).trim()) ?? defaultValue;

  /// Get environment variable as double
  ///
  /// Same priority order as [get]. Returns [defaultValue] when neither source
  /// has the key or the value is not a number.
  static double getDouble(String key, {double defaultValue = 0.0}) =>
      double.tryParse(get(key).trim()) ?? defaultValue;

  /// Check if an environment variable exists
  ///
  /// Returns true if the variable exists in .env file or --dart-define
  static bool has(String key) {
    // Check .env file (if loaded)
    if (_isInitialized) {
      try {
        final value = dotenv.maybeGet(key);
        if (value != null && value.isNotEmpty) {
          return true;
        }
      } on Exception {
        // Variable not found in .env
      }
    }
    // Check --dart-define flags
    return DartDefines.lookup(key).isNotEmpty;
  }

  /// Get all environment variables as a map
  ///
  /// Useful for debugging or logging configuration
  static Map<String, String> getAll() {
    final env = <String, String>{};

    // Add .env file values
    if (_isInitialized) {
      env.addAll(dotenv.env);
    }

    // Note: --dart-define values are compile-time only. They are not merged
    // in here; read them through [get]/[getBool]/[getInt]/[getDouble], which
    // consult the compile-time [DartDefines.values] table.
    return env;
  }

  /// Check if EnvConfig has been initialized
  static bool get isInitialized => _isInitialized;
}
// API_SEND_TIMEOUT and API_TIMEOUT were documented, parsed and never applied:
// ApiClient set only connect and receive
// timeouts, and nothing read AppConfig.apiTimeout. These tests load a `.env`
// with distinctive values and assert both reach the wire.

import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:convert';
import 'dart:typed_data';

import 'package:dio/dio.dart';
import 'package:flutter/services.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/config/app_config.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/config/env_config.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/contracts/network_contracts.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/errors/exceptions.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/adapters/dio_network_client.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/api_client.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/network/interceptors/auth_interceptor.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/storage/secure_storage_service.dart';
import 'package:flutter_starter/core/utils/result.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';
import 'package:mocktail/mocktail.dart';

import '../../helpers/in_memory_stores.dart';

const _assetChannel = 'flutter/assets';

class _MockSecureStorageService extends Mock implements SecureStorageService {}

/// A transport that never answers until its request is cancelled.
class _HangingAdapter implements HttpClientAdapter {
  int fetches = 0;
  bool cancelled = false;

  @override
  Future<ResponseBody> fetch(
    RequestOptions options,
    Stream<Uint8List>? requestStream,
    Future<void>? cancelFuture,
  ) {
    fetches++;
    final hang = Completer<ResponseBody>();
    unawaited(
      cancelFuture?.then((_) {
        cancelled = true;
        if (!hang.isCompleted) {
          hang.completeError(
            DioException.requestCancelled(
              requestOptions: options,
              reason: 'cancelled',
            ),
          );
        }
      }),
    );
    return hang.future;
  }

  @override
  void close({bool force = false}) {}
}

void main() {
  TestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();

  var environmentLoads = 0;
  Future<void> loadEnvironment(String values) async {
    final fileName = 'timeouts_env_${environmentLoads++}';
    TestDefaultBinaryMessengerBinding.instance.defaultBinaryMessenger
        .setMockMessageHandler(_assetChannel, (message) async {
          final key = utf8.decode(message!.buffer.asUint8List());
          if (key != fileName) return null;
          final bytes = Uint8List.fromList(utf8.encode(values));
          return ByteData.view(bytes.buffer);
        });
    await EnvConfig.load(fileName: fileName);
    expect(EnvConfig.isInitialized, isTrue);
  }

  setUp(() => loadEnvironment('API_TIMEOUT=1\nAPI_SEND_TIMEOUT=7\n'));

  tearDown(() {
    TestDefaultBinaryMessengerBinding.instance.defaultBinaryMessenger
        .setMockMessageHandler(_assetChannel, null);
  });

  ApiClient buildClient(HttpClientAdapter adapter) {
    return ApiClient(
      storageService: InMemoryStorage(),
      secureStorageService: _MockSecureStorageService(),
      authInterceptor: AuthInterceptor(
        tokenStore: InMemoryTokenStore(),
        refreshToken: () async => const Success('unused'),
      ),
    )..dio.httpClientAdapter = adapter;
  }

  group('criterion 1: API_SEND_TIMEOUT reaches both Dio instances', () {
    test('ApiClient sets sendTimeout from AppConfig.apiSendTimeout', () {
      final client = buildClient(_HangingAdapter());

      expect(client.dio.options.sendTimeout, const Duration(seconds: 7));
    });

    test('the 401 replay client borrows it from the host options', () {
      final authInterceptor = AuthInterceptor(
        tokenStore: InMemoryTokenStore(),
        refreshToken: () async => const Success('unused'),
      );
      ApiClient(
        storageService: InMemoryStorage(),
        secureStorageService: _MockSecureStorageService(),
        authInterceptor: authInterceptor,
      );

      expect(
        authInterceptor.retryDio().options.sendTimeout,
        const Duration(seconds: 7),
      );
    });

    test('a replay client with no host falls back to AppConfig', () {
      final authInterceptor = AuthInterceptor(
        tokenStore: InMemoryTokenStore(),
        refreshToken: () async => const Success('unused'),
      );

      expect(
        authInterceptor.retryDio().options.sendTimeout,
        const Duration(seconds: 7),
      );
    });
  });

  group('criterion 2: API_TIMEOUT is an end-to-end deadline per request', () {
    test('ApiClient gives up on a hanging request after API_TIMEOUT, as a '
        'timeout, and cancels it', () async {
      final adapter = _HangingAdapter();
      final client = buildClient(adapter);
      final watch = Stopwatch()..start();

      await expectLater(
        client.get('/slow'),
        throwsA(
          isA<NetworkException>().having(
            (e) => e.code,
            'code',
            'RECEIVE_TIMEOUT',
          ),
        ),
      );

      watch.stop();
      expect(watch.elapsed, greaterThanOrEqualTo(const Duration(seconds: 1)));
      expect(watch.elapsed, lessThan(const Duration(seconds: 5)));
      await pumpEventQueue();
      expect(adapter.cancelled, isTrue);
    });

    for (final seconds in [0, -1]) {
      test('API_TIMEOUT=$seconds disables the total deadline', () async {
        await loadEnvironment('API_TIMEOUT=$seconds\n');
        final adapter = _AnsweringAdapter(
          delay: const Duration(milliseconds: 50),
        );
        final client = buildClient(adapter);

        final response = await client.get('/delayed');

        expect(response.statusCode, 200);
        expect(response.data, {'ok': true});
        expect(adapter.fetches, 1);
      });
    }

    test(
      'shipped total and receive budgets both default to 30 seconds',
      () async {
        await loadEnvironment('API_SEND_TIMEOUT=7\n');
        final client = buildClient(_HangingAdapter());

        expect(AppConfig.apiTimeout, 30);
        expect(client.dio.options.receiveTimeout, const Duration(seconds: 30));
      },
    );

    test('DioNetworkClient without a deadline leaves the request alone', () {
      final adapter = _HangingAdapter();
      final dio = Dio()..httpClientAdapter = adapter;
      final client = DioNetworkClient(dio);

      final pending = client.send(
        const NetworkRequest(path: '/slow', method: NetworkMethod.get),
      );

      return expectLater(
        pending.timeout(const Duration(milliseconds: 300)),
        throwsA(isA<TimeoutException>()),
      );
    });

    test('a response inside the deadline is returned untouched', () async {
      final dio = Dio()
        ..httpClientAdapter = _AnsweringAdapter(
          delay: const Duration(milliseconds: 50),
        );
      final client = DioNetworkClient(
        dio,
        deadline: const Duration(seconds: 2),
      );

      final response = await client.send(
        const NetworkRequest(path: '/fast', method: NetworkMethod.get),
      );

      expect(response.statusCode, 200);
      expect(response.data, {'ok': true});
    });
  });
}

class _AnsweringAdapter implements HttpClientAdapter {
  _AnsweringAdapter({required this.delay});

  final Duration delay;
  int fetches = 0;

  @override
  Future<ResponseBody> fetch(
    RequestOptions options,
    Stream<Uint8List>? requestStream,
    Future<void>? cancelFuture,
  ) async {
    fetches++;
    await Future<void>.delayed(delay);
    return ResponseBody.fromString(
      jsonEncode({'ok': true}),
      200,
      headers: {
        Headers.contentTypeHeader: [Headers.jsonContentType],
      },
    );
  }

  @override
  void close({bool force = false}) {}
}
name: Build (Manual)

# Manual builds for every platform, plus one automatic job: on a PR that
# touches android/ (or this toolchain gate), `android-jdk25` checks the
# Gradle/AGP/Kotlin floors and builds a debug APK on JDK 25 - the JDK bundled
# with current Android Studio, which CI's Java 17 never exercised while the
# project could not build on it. The
# manual jobs keep their `inputs.platform` guards, which are false on a PR.
on:
  pull_request:
    branches: [ main ]
    paths:
      - 'android/**'
      - 'tool/check_android_toolchain.dart'
      - '.github/workflows/build.yml'
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      platform:
        description: 'Which platform(s) to build'
        required: true
        default: 'all'
        type: choice
        options:
          - all
          - android
          - ios
          - web
      # NOT a Gradle product flavor. This project configures itself through
      # `.env` layering and --dart-define (see docs/guides/configuration.md and
      # lib/core/config/app_config.dart, which reads ENVIRONMENT). No
      # productFlavors are declared in android/app/build.gradle.kts, so passing
      # --flavor made `flutter build apk` fail with
      # "Task 'assembleDevelopmentRelease' not found".
      environment:
        # No `development`: these jobs build release artifacts, and a release
        # build configured as development refuses to start (lib/main.dart).
        description: 'Value passed as --dart-define=ENVIRONMENT'
        required: true
        default: 'staging'
        type: choice
        options:
          - staging
          - production
      base_url:
        description: 'Override BASE_URL (optional)'
        required: false
        default: ''
      # Pinning is on in staging and production, and a release built with
      # pinning on and no fingerprints refuses to start (lib/main.dart).
      # Empty falls back to the repository variable API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS.
      ssl_fingerprints:
        description: 'API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS (empty: the repository variable)'
        required: false
        default: ''
      ssl_pinning:
        description: 'off builds without pinning (ENABLE_SSL_PINNING=false)'
        required: true
        default: 'on'
        type: choice
        options:
          - 'on'
          - 'off'

concurrency:
  group: build-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}-${{ inputs.platform }}-${{ inputs.environment }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  android-jdk25:
    if: >-
      github.event_name == 'pull_request' ||
      inputs.platform == 'all' || inputs.platform == 'android'
    name: Android on JDK 25 (toolchain floor + debug build)
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1

      - name: Setup Flutter
        uses: subosito/flutter-action@1a449444c387b1966244ae4d4f8c696479add0b2 # v2.23.0
        with:
          channel: 'stable'
          cache: true

      - name: Setup Java
        uses: actions/setup-java@77dd5b2ce225ea11469928ac317850245a74ae78 # v6.2.0
        with:
          distribution: 'temurin'
          java-version: '25'

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: flutter pub get

      # The bundled-secrets guard, before any build: a `.env` declared as a
      # Flutter asset ships inside the artifact.
      - name: Guard against a bundled secrets file
        run: dart run tool/check_env_assets.dart

      # Fails, naming the version, when Gradle, AGP or Kotlin is below the
      # floor stated once in tool/check_android_toolchain.dart.
      - name: Check Gradle, AGP and Kotlin against Flutter's floor
        run: dart run tool/check_android_toolchain.dart

      # Flutter only warns when a version is on its way out; a warning here
      # means the floor above is behind Flutter's, so it fails too.
      - name: Build debug APK
        shell: bash
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          java -version 2>&1 | head -1
          flutter build apk --debug 2>&1 | tee build-apk.log
          if grep -qi 'will soon be dropped' build-apk.log; then
            echo "::error::Flutter warned that a toolchain version will soon be dropped; raise it and the floor in tool/check_android_toolchain.dart"
            exit 1
          fi

  build-android:
    if: inputs.platform == 'all' || inputs.platform == 'android'
    name: Build Android
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1

      - name: Setup Flutter
        uses: subosito/flutter-action@1a449444c387b1966244ae4d4f8c696479add0b2 # v2.23.0
        with:
          channel: 'stable'
          cache: true

      - name: Setup Java
        uses: actions/setup-java@77dd5b2ce225ea11469928ac317850245a74ae78 # v6.2.0
        with:
          distribution: 'temurin'
          java-version: '17'

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: flutter pub get

      # The bundled-secrets guard, before any build: a `.env` declared as a
      # Flutter asset ships inside the artifact.
      - name: Guard against a bundled secrets file
        run: dart run tool/check_env_assets.dart

      # `flutter build apk` defaults to --release, and android/app/build.gradle.kts
      # now refuses to sign a release with the debug keystore. This job is a
      # throwaway smoke build, so it mints a throwaway key and follows exactly the
      # key.properties recipe documented in deploy-android.yml. The resulting APK
      # is NOT distributable - it is signed by a key that exists only for the
      # lifetime of this runner.
      - name: Generate throwaway release keystore
        shell: bash
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          keytool -genkeypair -v \
            -keystore android/ci-throwaway-keystore.jks \
            -storetype JKS \
            -storepass ci-throwaway -keypass ci-throwaway \
            -alias ci-throwaway \
            -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 30 \
            -dname "CN=CI Throwaway, OU=flutter-starter, O=flutter-starter, C=US"
          {
            echo "storeFile=ci-throwaway-keystore.jks"
            echo "storePassword=ci-throwaway"
            echo "keyAlias=ci-throwaway"
            echo "keyPassword=ci-throwaway"
          } > android/key.properties

      - name: Build APK
        shell: bash
        # Inputs reach the script through env:, never by ${{ }} substitution
        # into its text: base_url is free text, and substituted it would run
        # as code.
        env:
          INPUT_ENVIRONMENT: ${{ inputs.environment }}
          INPUT_BASE_URL: ${{ inputs.base_url }}
          API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS: ${{ inputs.ssl_fingerprints || vars.API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS }}
          ENABLE_SSL_PINNING: ${{ inputs.ssl_pinning == 'off' && 'false' || '' }}
        run: |
          ENVIRONMENT="$INPUT_ENVIRONMENT"
          BASE_URL="$INPUT_BASE_URL"
          # A staging or production release refuses a non-https BASE_URL
          # (lib/main.dart), so the fallback is AppConfig's https default.
          if [ -z "$BASE_URL" ]; then
            case "$ENVIRONMENT" in
              staging) BASE_URL="https://api-staging.example.com" ;;
              *) BASE_URL="https://api.example.com" ;;
            esac
          fi

          # Refuses a staging or production build with no fingerprints and
          # no explicit opt-out, before building.
          . scripts/ci/ssl_pinning_defines.sh
          ssl_pinning_defines "$ENVIRONMENT" || exit 1

          flutter build apk \
            --dart-define=ENVIRONMENT="$ENVIRONMENT" \
            --dart-define=BASE_URL="$BASE_URL" \
            ${SSL_PINNING_DEFINES[@]+"${SSL_PINNING_DEFINES[@]}"}

      # Proves the APK is signed, and signed by the throwaway CI identity minted
      # above rather than by the Android debug key. The check itself lives in
      # scripts/ci/verify_apk_signer.sh so it can be pointed at a deliberately
      # debug-signed or deliberately unsigned APK and observed to FAIL - the
      # inline version could not be, and shipped broken twice as a result.
      - name: Print APK signer certificate
        shell: bash
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          APK=$(find build/app/outputs/flutter-apk -name '*.apk' | sort | head -1)
          ./scripts/ci/verify_apk_signer.sh "$APK"

      - name: Upload artifacts
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@cf430e030ddbb5b0abf93d22962f4752f3646cd9 # v7.0.2
        with:
          name: android-apks-${{ inputs.environment }}
          path: build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/*.apk

  build-ios:
    if: inputs.platform == 'all' || inputs.platform == 'ios'
    name: Build iOS (no codesign)
    runs-on: macos-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1

      - name: Setup Flutter
        uses: subosito/flutter-action@1a449444c387b1966244ae4d4f8c696479add0b2 # v2.23.0
        with:
          channel: 'stable'
          cache: true

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: flutter pub get

      # The bundled-secrets guard, before any build: a `.env` declared as a
      # Flutter asset ships inside the artifact.
      - name: Guard against a bundled secrets file
        run: dart run tool/check_env_assets.dart

      - name: Install CocoaPods
        run: |
          cd ios
          pod install

      - name: Build iOS
        shell: bash
        # Inputs reach the script through env:, never by ${{ }} substitution
        # into its text: base_url is free text, and substituted it would run
        # as code.
        env:
          INPUT_ENVIRONMENT: ${{ inputs.environment }}
          INPUT_BASE_URL: ${{ inputs.base_url }}
          API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS: ${{ inputs.ssl_fingerprints || vars.API_SSL_FINGERPRINTS }}
          ENABLE_SSL_PINNING: ${{ inputs.ssl_pinning == 'off' && 'false' || '' }}
        run: |
          ENVIRONMENT="$INPUT_ENVIRONMENT"
          BASE_URL="$INPUT_BASE_URL"
          # A staging or production release refuses a non-https BASE_URL
          # (lib/main.dart), so the fallback is AppConfig's https default.
          if [ -z "$BASE_URL" ]; then
            case "$ENVIRONMENT" in
              staging) BASE_URL="https://api-staging.example.com" ;;
              *) BASE_URL="https://api.example.com" ;;
            esac
          fi

          # Refuses a staging or production build with no fingerprints and
          # no explicit opt-out, before building.
          . scripts/ci/ssl_pinning_defines.sh
          ssl_pinning_defines "$ENVIRONMENT" || exit 1

          flutter build ios \
            --no-codesign \
            --dart-define=ENVIRONMENT="$ENVIRONMENT" \
            --dart-define=BASE_URL="$BASE_URL" \
            ${SSL_PINNING_DEFINES[@]+"${SSL_PINNING_DEFINES[@]}"}

  build-web:
    if: inputs.platform == 'all' || inputs.platform == 'web'
    name: Build Web
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1

      - name: Setup Flutter
        uses: subosito/flutter-action@1a449444c387b1966244ae4d4f8c696479add0b2 # v2.23.0
        with:
          channel: 'stable'
          cache: true

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: flutter pub get

      # The bundled-secrets guard, before any build: a `.env` declared as a
      # Flutter asset ships inside the artifact.
      - name: Guard against a bundled secrets file
        run: dart run tool/check_env_assets.dart

      - name: Build Web
        shell: bash
        # Inputs reach the script through env:, never by ${{ }} substitution
        # into its text: base_url is free text, and substituted it would run
        # as code.
        env:
          INPUT_ENVIRONMENT: ${{ inputs.environment }}
          INPUT_BASE_URL: ${{ inputs.base_url }}
        run: |
          ENVIRONMENT="$INPUT_ENVIRONMENT"
          BASE_URL="$INPUT_BASE_URL"
          # A staging or production release refuses a non-https BASE_URL
          # (lib/main.dart), so the fallback is AppConfig's https default.
          if [ -z "$BASE_URL" ]; then
            case "$ENVIRONMENT" in
              staging) BASE_URL="https://api-staging.example.com" ;;
              *) BASE_URL="https://api.example.com" ;;
            esac
          fi

          flutter build web \
            --dart-define=ENVIRONMENT="$ENVIRONMENT" \
            --dart-define=BASE_URL="$BASE_URL"

      - name: Upload artifacts
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@cf430e030ddbb5b0abf93d22962f4752f3646cd9 # v7.0.2
        with:
          name: web-build-${{ inputs.environment }}
          path: build/web

name: Coverage Analysis

# ========================================================================
# Workflow Triggers Configuration
# ========================================================================
# This is a starter template, so branches are commented out by default.
# Uncomment and configure the branches according to your project needs:
#
# For example:
#   push:
#     branches: [ main, develop ]
#   pull_request:
#     branches: [ main, develop ]
#
# Or for a single branch:
#   push:
#     branches: [ main ]
#   pull_request:
#     branches: [ main ]
#
# workflow_dispatch allows manual triggering from GitHub Actions UI
# ========================================================================
on:
  workflow_dispatch:  # Allows manual trigger from GitHub Actions UI
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 3 * * 1' # weekly (Mon 03:00 UTC)

concurrency:
  group: coverage-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  coverage-analysis:
    name: Coverage Analysis
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    # The coverage targets, in one place. The enforcement step and the job
    # summary both read these, so the table can no longer promise a target the
    # gate does not enforce.
    #
    # Domain is 100%: keep it there by writing namespace classes as
    # `abstract final class X { static const ... }` rather than with a private
    # `X._()` constructor, which no test can cover.
    env:
      COVERAGE_MIN_TOTAL: 80
      COVERAGE_MIN_DOMAIN: 100
      COVERAGE_MIN_DATA: 90
      COVERAGE_MIN_PRESENTATION: 80
      COVERAGE_MIN_CORE: 80

    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1
        with:
          fetch-depth: 1
      
      - name: Setup Flutter
        uses: subosito/flutter-action@1a449444c387b1966244ae4d4f8c696479add0b2 # v2.23.0
        with:
          # No `flutter-version:` here. "stable" is a CHANNEL, not a version, and
          # passing it as a version makes flutter-action fail with "Unable to
          # determine Flutter version for channel: stable version: stable".
          # ci.yml sets only `channel:`, which is why ci.yml has always worked.
          channel: 'stable'
          cache: true
      
      - name: Install dependencies
        run: flutter pub get
      
      # Every library under lib/ in lcov.info, with zero hits where no test ran
      # it; otherwise an untested file never moved a layer's figure.
      - name: Import every library for coverage
        run: dart run tool/coverage_helper.dart

      - name: Run tests with coverage
        timeout-minutes: 20
        run: flutter test --coverage --timeout=5m
      
      - name: Install lcov
        run: |
          sudo apt-get update
          sudo apt-get install -y lcov bc

      - name: Remove generated files from coverage
        run: |
          lcov --remove coverage/lcov.info --ignore-errors unused \
            '**/*.g.dart' \
            '**/*.freezed.dart' \
            '**/*.config.dart' \
            '**/generated/**' \
            '**/l10n/**' \
            '**/main.dart' \
            'test/**' \
            '**/*_test.dart' \
            '**/test_helpers.dart' \
            '**/test_fixtures.dart' \
            -o coverage/lcov.info
      
      - name: Generate coverage report
        run: |
          genhtml coverage/lcov.info -o coverage/html --no-function-coverage

      - name: Calculate coverage by layer (architecture gates)
        id: layer_coverage
        run: |
          chmod +x scripts/test/calculate_layer_coverage.sh

          # --github-output emits ONLY key=value lines. The previous version
          # appended the script's human summary too, so prose like
          # "Summary of coverage by layer" became a step-output key.
          ./scripts/test/calculate_layer_coverage.sh --github-output coverage/lcov.info \
            >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

          # And print the readable form for whoever is reading the log.
          ./scripts/test/calculate_layer_coverage.sh coverage/lcov.info

      - name: Calculate coverage metrics
        id: metrics
        run: |
          # Overall coverage
          OVERALL=$(lcov --summary coverage/lcov.info 2>&1 | grep "lines.*:" | head -1 | grep -oP '\d+\.\d+%' | head -1 | sed 's/%//' || echo "0")
          
          # File counts, kept only as context. They are NOT coverage - labelling
          # them "Domain files: 15" beside a percentage once made a bogus
          # coverage report look like a real defect. Per-layer percentages come
          # from the
          # layer_coverage step above.
          DOMAIN_FILES=$(grep -c "^SF:.*lib/features/.*/domain" coverage/lcov.info || echo "0")
          DATA_FILES=$(grep -c "^SF:.*lib/features/.*/data" coverage/lcov.info || echo "0")
          PRESENTATION_FILES=$(grep -c "^SF:.*lib/features/.*/presentation" coverage/lcov.info || echo "0")
          CORE_FILES=$(grep -c "^SF:.*lib/core" coverage/lcov.info || echo "0")

          {
            echo "overall=$OVERALL"
            echo "domain_files=$DOMAIN_FILES"
            echo "data_files=$DATA_FILES"
            echo "presentation_files=$PRESENTATION_FILES"
            echo "core_files=$CORE_FILES"
          } >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

          echo "📊 Overall line coverage: ${OVERALL}%"
          echo ""
          echo "Source files measured per layer (counts, not coverage):"
          echo "  domain: ${DOMAIN_FILES}   data: ${DATA_FILES}   presentation: ${PRESENTATION_FILES}   core: ${CORE_FILES}"
          echo "Per-layer coverage percentages are reported by the"
          echo "'Calculate coverage by layer (architecture gates)' step above."

      # Written before enforcement, so a failing gate still shows the table.
      - name: Write coverage summary
        if: always() && steps.layer_coverage.outcome == 'success'
        run: |
          {
            echo "## Coverage Analysis"
            echo ""
            echo "| Layer | Coverage | Target |"
            echo "|-------|----------|--------|"
            echo "| Total | ${{ steps.metrics.outputs.overall }}% | ${COVERAGE_MIN_TOTAL}% |"
            echo "| Domain | ${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.domain }}% | ${COVERAGE_MIN_DOMAIN}% |"
            echo "| Data | ${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.data }}% | ${COVERAGE_MIN_DATA}% |"
            echo "| Presentation | ${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.presentation }}% | ${COVERAGE_MIN_PRESENTATION}% |"
            echo "| Core | ${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.core }}% | ${COVERAGE_MIN_CORE}% |"
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

      - name: Enforce coverage thresholds
        run: |
          OVERALL=${{ steps.metrics.outputs.overall }}
          DOMAIN_COV=${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.domain }}
          DATA_COV=${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.data }}
          PRESENTATION_COV=${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.presentation }}
          CORE_COV=${{ steps.layer_coverage.outputs.core }}

          FAILED=0

          check_threshold() {
            local name=$1
            local current=$2
            local target=$3
            # bc prints nothing for a non-number, and an empty (( )) is
            # false, which would read as a pass.
            local number='^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$'
            if ! [[ "$current" =~ $number && "$target" =~ $number ]]; then
              echo "❌ $name: not a number (coverage '${current}', target '${target}')"
              return 1
            fi
            if (( $(echo "$current < $target" | bc -l) )); then
              echo "❌ $name: ${current}% < ${target}%"
              return 1
            else
              echo "✅ $name: ${current}% >= ${target}%"
              return 0
            fi
          }

          check_threshold "Total Coverage" "$OVERALL" "$COVERAGE_MIN_TOTAL" || exit 1
          check_threshold "Domain Layer" "$DOMAIN_COV" "$COVERAGE_MIN_DOMAIN" || FAILED=1
          check_threshold "Data Layer" "$DATA_COV" "$COVERAGE_MIN_DATA" || FAILED=1
          check_threshold "Presentation Layer" "$PRESENTATION_COV" "$COVERAGE_MIN_PRESENTATION" || FAILED=1
          check_threshold "Core Layer" "$CORE_COV" "$COVERAGE_MIN_CORE" || FAILED=1

          if [ $FAILED -eq 1 ]; then
            echo "⚠️ One or more architectural layers did not meet coverage targets."
            exit 1
          fi
      
      # ========================================================================
      # Codecov Upload Configuration
      # ========================================================================
      # Choose ONE option based on your repository visibility:
      #
      # OPTION 1: PRIVATE REPOSITORY (requires CODECOV_TOKEN)
      # - Uncomment the block below
      # - Add CODECOV_TOKEN to GitHub Secrets (Settings > Secrets > Actions)
      # - Get token from: https://codecov.io
      #
      # OPTION 2: PUBLIC REPOSITORY (no token needed)
      # - Comment out the block below
      # - Uncomment the public repository block
      # ========================================================================
      
      # PRIVATE REPOSITORY: Upload to Codecov (requires token)
      # Comment out the block below if your repository is PUBLIC
      # - name: Upload to Codecov (Private)
      #   uses: codecov/codecov-action@303a32d7a59b442fa8d48b6a1cc6825c09c847a5 # v7.1.1
      #   with:
      #     files: ./coverage/lcov.info
      #     flags: unittests
      #     name: codecov-umbrella
      #     fail_ci_if_error: false
      #     token: ${{ secrets.CODECOV_TOKEN }}
      
      # PUBLIC REPOSITORY: Upload to Codecov (no token needed)
      - name: Upload to Codecov (Public)
        uses: codecov/codecov-action@303a32d7a59b442fa8d48b6a1cc6825c09c847a5 # v7.1.1
        with:
          files: ./coverage/lcov.info
          flags: unittests
          name: codecov-umbrella
          fail_ci_if_error: false
          # No token needed for public repositories

